        "polynomial-interpolation-and-remainder",
        "fredholm-elliptic-problems-and-the-elliptic-spectrum",
        "sparse-high-dimensional-estimation-and-covariance"
      ],
      "description": "{\"scope\": [\"Define positive-definite radial kernel interpolation at distinct sites, and conditionally positive-definite augmented system with polynomial side conditions and unisolvent sites.\", \"Prove uniqueness via quadratic form and polynomial unisolvence; define native-space interpolant and power function P_X(x), prove |f(x)-s_X f(x)| <= P_X(x)||f||_H for f in the native space.\", \"For one fixed Wendland or Matérn kernel prove native Sobolev equivalence and fill-distance/separation error estimate on an explicit regular domain; Gaussian flat-shape illconditioning and stable RBF-QR are separate numerical claims, with a defined stable basis algorithm and proved change-of-basis before promising stability.\", \"Define RBF-FD weights by augmented local moment solve; prove polynomial reproduction/local truncation only. PDE stability is owned by PDE consumer.\"], \"proof_route\": \"RKHS projection/Cauchy-Schwarz and saddle-system nullspace argument; Fourier characterization/local zeros lemma is fully local prerequisite; finite moment expansion for RBF-FD.\", \"examples\": [\"Scattered interpolation of smooth surface\", \"Duplicate-site singularity\", \"Small fill distance versus bad separation/conditioning\"]}",
      "items": [
  {
    "id": "def-rbf-kernels-sites-and-augmented-interpolation",
    "kind": "definition",
    "title": "Radial kernels, polynomial augmentation, and site geometry",
    "deps": [],
    "justified_by": [
      "lem-rbf-augmented-system-is-invertible"
    ],
    "dependency_level": 0,
    "provenance": {
      "statement": "literature-derived",
      "proof": "not-applicable"
    },
    "sources": {
      "references": [
        {
          "title": "Narcowich, Ward, and Wendland, Sobolev bounds on functions with scattered zeros, Math. Comp. 74 (2005), §§1 and 3.1",
          "url": "https://www.researchgate.net/publication/220577178_Sobolev_bounds_on_functions_with_scattered_zeros_with_applications_to_radial_basis_function_surface_fitting"
        },
        {
          "title": "Rasmussen and Williams, Gaussian Processes for Machine Learning, Chapter 4",
          "url": "https://gaussianprocess.org/gpml/chapters/RW4.pdf"
        }
      ]
    },
    "statement": "Fix an integer $d\\ge1$. An admitted domain $\\Omega\\subset\\mathbb R^d$ is a nonempty bounded Lipschitz domain (its boundary is locally the graph of a Lipschitz function, with $\\Omega$ on one side) with fixed $r_0>0$ and $0<\\theta<\\pi/2$ such that for every $x\\in\\overline\\Omega$ there is an orthogonal map $Q_x$ for which\n$$x+Q_x\\{z\\in\\mathbb R^d:0<|z|<r_0,\\ z_1>|z|\\cos\\theta\\}\\subset\\Omega.$$\nAn orthogonal map is a real linear map with $Q_x^{\\mathsf T}Q_x=I$, equivalently one preserving the Euclidean inner product. The map $Q_x$ may depend on $x$; no simultaneous selection of the maps is part of this definition.\n\nA real radial kernel on $\\mathbb R^d$ has the form $K(x,y)=\\phi(|x-y|)$ for a function $\\phi:[0,\\infty)\\to\\mathbb R$. For a finite ordered list of distinct sites $X=(x_1,\\ldots,x_N)$, its Gram matrix is $K_X=(K(x_i,x_j))_{i,j=1}^N$. The kernel is **strictly positive definite** if $c^{\\mathsf T}K_Xc>0$ for every such $X$ and every nonzero $c\\in\\mathbb R^N$; the nonstrict positive-semidefinite condition replaces $>$ by $\\ge$. Only strict positivity is used for the ordinary interpolation solve below.\n\nFor an integer $m\\ge1$, let $\\Pi_{m-1}(\\mathbb R^d)$ be the real polynomials of total degree at most $m-1$, with an ordered basis $p_1,\\ldots,p_J$, where $J=\\dim\\Pi_{m-1}(\\mathbb R^d)$. The evaluation matrix on $X$ is $P_X=(p_j(x_i))_{i,j}$. The sites are **$m$-unisolvent** for this polynomial space when $P_X$ has linearly independent columns. A kernel is **strictly conditionally positive definite of order $m$** when, for every finite distinct $X$ and this evaluation matrix, $c^{\\mathsf T}K_Xc>0$ for every nonzero $c\\in\\mathbb R^N$ satisfying $P_X^{\\mathsf T}c=0$. Thus strict conditional positivity is a strict inequality on the moment-null subspace; it is stronger than the corresponding nonstrict inequality.\n\nFor data $y\\in\\mathbb R^N$, the strictly positive definite interpolation system is $K_Xa=y$. For an order-$m$ strictly conditionally positive definite kernel and $m$-unisolvent sites, the polynomially augmented interpolation system is\n$$\\begin{bmatrix}K_X&P_X\\\\P_X^{\\mathsf T}&0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}a\\\\b\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}y\\\\0\\end{bmatrix}.$$\nWhenever the relevant system has a unique solution, its kernel-plus-polynomial function $x\\mapsto\\sum_i a_iK(x,x_i)+\\sum_j b_jp_j(x)$ is called the interpolant. Invertibility for the stated strict hypotheses is supplied by [[lem-rbf-augmented-system-is-invertible]].\n\nFor a finite nonempty site set $X\\subset\\Omega$, the **fill distance** is $h_X=\\sup_{x\\in\\Omega}\\min_{x_i\\in X}|x-x_i|$. If $N\\ge2$, the **separation radius** and **mesh ratio** are $q_X=\\tfrac12\\min_{i\\ne j}|x_i-x_j|$ and $\\rho_X=h_X/q_X$, respectively. These are exact geometric quantities, independent of any floating-point solver."
  },
  {
    "id": "lem-rbf-augmented-system-is-invertible",
    "kind": "lemma",
    "title": "Strict positive and conditionally positive augmented interpolation systems are invertible",
    "deps": [
      "def-rbf-kernels-sites-and-augmented-interpolation",
      "thm-rank-nullity",
      "thm-reals-field",
      "def-invertible-matrix-and-general-linear-group"
    ],
    "dependency_level": 1,
    "provenance": {
      "statement": "literature-derived",
      "proof": "ai-altered"
    },
    "sources": {
      "references": [
        {
          "title": "Narcowich, Ward, and Wendland, Sobolev bounds on functions with scattered zeros, Math. Comp. 74 (2005), §3.1",
          "url": "https://www.researchgate.net/publication/220577178_Sobolev_bounds_on_functions_with_scattered_zeros_with_applications_to_radial_basis_function_surface_fitting"
        },
        {
          "title": "Gregory E. Fasshauer, MATH 590: Meshfree Methods, Chapter 13, pp. 18–25",
          "url": "https://math.iit.edu/~fass/Notes590_Ch13Print.pdf"
        }
      ]
    },
    "statement": "Let $X=(x_1,\\ldots,x_N)$ be a finite ordered list of distinct sites in $\\mathbb R^d$. If $K$ is strictly positive definite, then $K_X$ is invertible and $K_Xa=y$ has a unique solution for every $y\\in\\mathbb R^N$. If $K$ is strictly conditionally positive definite of order $m$ and $X$ is $m$-unisolvent for $\\Pi_{m-1}(\\mathbb R^d)$, then the augmented matrix in [[def-rbf-kernels-sites-and-augmented-interpolation]] is invertible and its system has a unique solution for every right-hand side."
  },
  {
    "id": "def-normalized-matern-spectral-kernel",
    "kind": "definition",
    "title": "The normalized Matérn kernel in the 2π Fourier convention",
    "deps": [
      "def-rbf-kernels-sites-and-augmented-interpolation",
      "def-real-gamma-function-by-the-euler-integral",
      "def-lebesgue-measure-and-the-lebesgue-sigma-algebra",
      "def-countable-choice"
    ],
    "justified_by": [
      "lem-matern-spectral-integral-and-normalization"
    ],
    "dependency_level": 1,
    "provenance": {
      "statement": "literature-derived",
      "proof": "not-applicable"
    },
    "sources": {
      "references": [
        {
          "title": "Rasmussen and Williams, Gaussian Processes for Machine Learning, Chapter 4, equations (4.14)–(4.15)",
          "url": "https://gaussianprocess.org/gpml/chapters/RW4.pdf"
        },
        {
          "title": "Gregory E. Fasshauer, MATH 590: Meshfree Methods, Chapter 13, pp. 34–36",
          "url": "https://math.iit.edu/~fass/Notes590_Ch13Print.pdf"
        }
      ]
    },
    "statement": "Fix the dimension $d\\ge1$ of an admitted domain, a smoothness parameter $\\nu>0$, and an inverse length $a>0$. Assume Countable Choice for the Lebesgue-integration suppliers used to justify this formula; this definition does not assume full Choice. Put $s=\\nu+d/2$ and\n$$A=\\frac{\\Gamma(s)(4\\pi)^{d/2}a^{2\\nu}}{\\Gamma(\\nu)},\\qquad w(\\xi)=A(a^2+4\\pi^2|\\xi|^2)^{-s}.$$\nAll integrals are Lebesgue integrals with respect to $\\lambda_d$ ([[def-lebesgue-measure-and-the-lebesgue-sigma-algebra]]), and $\\Gamma$ is the Euler integral of [[def-real-gamma-function-by-the-euler-integral]]. With the negative-sign Fourier transform convention $\\widehat U(\\xi)=\\int U(x)e^{-2\\pi i x\\cdot\\xi}\\,dx$, define the normalized stationary kernel by\n$$k(z)=\\int_{\\mathbb R^d}w(\\xi)e^{2\\pi i z\\cdot\\xi}\\,d\\xi,\\qquad K(x,y)=k(x-y).$$\nThe corresponding length parameter in the usual Matérn notation is $\\ell=\\sqrt{2\\nu}/a$. For $b>0$, define the modified Bessel function in its integral normalization by\n$$K_\\nu(b)=\\frac12\\left(\\frac b2\\right)^\\nu\\int_0^\\infty u^{-\\nu-1}e^{-u-b^2/(4u)}\\,du.$$\nThe classical Bessel expression for $k(z)$ when $z\\ne0$, its value $k(0)=1$, absolute convergence, continuity, and radial symmetry are established by [[lem-matern-spectral-integral-and-normalization]]. This definition makes no finite-precision stability claim."
  },
  {
    "id": "def-bessel-potential-space-and-restriction-quotient",
    "kind": "definition",
    "title": "Fourier Bessel-potential and restriction-quotient Sobolev spaces",
    "deps": [
      "def-rbf-kernels-sites-and-augmented-interpolation",
      "def-lebesgue-measure-and-the-lebesgue-sigma-algebra",
      "def-l-p-space-as-a-quotient-by-null-functions",
      "def-complex-lp-and-euclidean-test-function-conventions",
      "def-real-and-complex-inner-product-space",
      "def-real-power",
      "def-axiom-of-choice",
      "def-countable-choice"
    ],
    "justified_by": [
      "lem-matern-weighted-fourier-space-is-hilbert-and-injective",
      "lem-schwartz-dense-in-fourier-bessel-potential-space",
      "lem-restricted-matern-native-space-is-sobolev"
    ],
    "dependency_level": 1,
    "provenance": {
      "statement": "literature-derived",
      "proof": "not-applicable"
    },
    "sources": {
      "references": [
        {
          "title": "Gregory E. Fasshauer, MATH 590: Meshfree Methods, Chapter 13, pp. 23–36",
          "url": "https://math.iit.edu/~fass/Notes590_Ch13Print.pdf"
        },
        {
          "title": "Rasmussen and Williams, Gaussian Processes for Machine Learning, Chapter 4, equations (4.5)–(4.6)",
          "url": "https://gaussianprocess.org/gpml/chapters/RW4.pdf"
        }
      ]
    },
    "statement": "Assume the Axiom of Choice when using the well-definedness results below; it is inherited conservatively from the published $L^2$ completeness supplier. Let $d\\ge1$ and $s>d/2$. Write $L^2_s(\\mathbb R^d)$ for the equivalence classes modulo Lebesgue-almost-everywhere equality of measurable complex functions $g$ satisfying\n$$\\|g\\|_{L^2_s}^2=\\int_{\\mathbb R^d}(1+|\\xi|^2)^s|g(\\xi)|^2\\,d\\xi<\\infty.$$\nFor such $g$, the **inverse Fourier representative** is the function\n$$U_g(x)=\\int_{\\mathbb R^d}g(\\xi)e^{2\\pi i x\\cdot\\xi}\\,d\\xi.$$\nThe whole-space Bessel-potential space $H^s(\\mathbb R^d)$ consists of these representatives, with norm $\\|U_g\\|_{H^s(\\mathbb R^d)}=\\|g\\|_{L^2_s}$. For the admitted domain $\\Omega$ of [[def-rbf-kernels-sites-and-augmented-interpolation]], define $H^s(\\Omega)$ to be the restrictions $U|_\\Omega$ of whole-space members, with the quotient norm\n$$\\|u\\|_{H^s(\\Omega)}=\\inf\\{\\|U\\|_{H^s(\\mathbb R^d)}:U|_\\Omega=u\\}.$$\nThe whole-space inner product is $\\langle U_g,U_h\\rangle_{H^s}=\\int (1+|\\xi|^2)^s g(\\xi)\\overline{h(\\xi)}\\,d\\xi$, linear in the first variable as in [[def-real-and-complex-inner-product-space]].\nThe inverse Fourier map is injective, whole-space representatives are continuous, the whole-space model is Hilbert, and Schwartz functions are dense in that norm; these facts are supplied by [[lem-matern-weighted-fourier-space-is-hilbert-and-injective]] and [[lem-schwartz-dense-in-fourier-bessel-potential-space]]. The restriction quotient is a norm by bounded point evaluation; its Hilbert realization as the restricted native space is proved in [[lem-restricted-matern-native-space-is-sobolev]]. No extension operator or identification with an intrinsic fractional Sobolev norm on $\\Omega$ is part of this definition."
  },
  {
    "id": "lem-cone-sites-admit-stable-polynomial-norming-stencils",
    "kind": "lemma",
    "title": "Uniform cone domains admit stable polynomial norming stencils",
    "deps": [
      "def-rbf-kernels-sites-and-augmented-interpolation",
      "thm-rank-nullity",
      "thm-reals-field",
      "thm-reals-ordered-field",
      "thm-root-bound-for-polynomials-over-a-domain"
    ],
    "dependency_level": 1,
    "provenance": {
      "statement": "literature-derived",
      "proof": "ai-altered"
    },
    "sources": {
      "references": [
        {
          "title": "Narcowich, Ward, and Wendland, Sobolev bounds on functions with scattered zeros, Math. Comp. 74 (2005), §§2.1–2.2",
          "url": "https://www.researchgate.net/publication/220577178_Sobolev_bounds_on_functions_with_scattered_zeros_with_applications_to_radial_basis_function_surface_fitting"
        }
      ]
    },
    "statement": "Let $\\Omega\\subset\\mathbb R^d$ be an admitted domain of [[def-rbf-kernels-sites-and-augmented-interpolation]], let $X\\subset\\Omega$ be finite and nonempty, and put $h=h_X$. Fix an integer $m\\ge1$ and let $J=\\dim\\Pi_{m-1}(\\mathbb R^d)$. There are constants $R,B,h_0>0$, depending only on $d,m,r_0,\\theta$, such that if $0<h\\le h_0$, then for every $x\\in\\overline\\Omega$ there are distinct nodes $y_1,\\ldots,y_J\\in X$ and real weights $\\lambda_1,\\ldots,\\lambda_J$ with\n$$|y_j-x|\\le Rh,\\qquad \\sum_{j=1}^J|\\lambda_j|\\le B,\\qquad p(x)=\\sum_{j=1}^J\\lambda_jp(y_j)\\quad(p\\in\\Pi_{m-1}(\\mathbb R^d)).$$\nNo lower bound on site separation is assumed."
  },
  {
    "id": "lem-matern-spectral-integral-and-normalization",
    "kind": "lemma",
    "title": "The Matérn spectral integral, normalization, and radial formula",
    "deps": [
      "def-normalized-matern-spectral-kernel",
