      "items": [
        {
          "id": "def-piecewise-cubic-hermite-interpolant",
          "kind": "definition",
          "deps": [
            "def-integer-power"
          ],
          "dependency_level": 0,
          "title": "Piecewise cubic Hermite interpolant",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "literature-derived",
            "proof": "not-applicable"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "Carl R. de Boor, A Practical Guide to Splines, revised edition, Chapter IV, pp. 39–40",
                "url": "https://djvu.online/file/W5YE3VGOgt1Ml"
              }
            ]
          },
          "statement": "Let $N\\ge1$ be an integer, let $x_0<\\cdots<x_N$ be real nodes, let $y_0,\\ldots,y_N\\in\\mathbb R$ be values, and let $m_0,\\ldots,m_N\\in\\mathbb R$ be prescribed slopes. For $0\\le i<N$ put $h_i=x_{i+1}-x_i>0$ and $t=(x-x_i)/h_i$ on $[x_i,x_{i+1}]$. Define\n$$H_{00}(t)=2t^3-3t^2+1,\\quad H_{01}(t)=-2t^3+3t^2,\\quad H_{10}(t)=t^3-2t^2+t,\\quad H_{11}(t)=t^3-t^2.$$\nThe cubic Hermite piece on $[x_i,x_{i+1}]$ is\n$$P_i(x)=y_iH_{00}(t)+y_{i+1}H_{01}(t)+h_im_iH_{10}(t)+h_im_{i+1}H_{11}(t).$$\nThe piecewise cubic Hermite interpolant is the function whose restriction to each such interval is $P_i$; adjacent pieces use their common prescribed value $y_i$ at a shared node. The slopes $m_i$ are input data, not values inferred from neighboring pieces."
        },
        {
          "id": "def-piecewise-bernstein-representation",
          "kind": "definition",
          "deps": [
            "def-binomial-coefficient",
            "def-canonical-natural",
            "def-finite-sum",
            "def-integer-power"
          ],
          "dependency_level": 0,
          "title": "Piecewise Bernstein representation",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "literature-derived",
            "proof": "not-applicable"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "Rida T. Farouki, The Bernstein polynomial basis: a centennial retrospective, Computer Aided Geometric Design 29 (2012), pp. 379–419",
                "url": "https://faculty.engineering.ucdavis.edu/farouki/wp-content/uploads/sites/51/2021/07/Bernstein-polynomial-basis.pdf"
              }
            ]
          },
          "statement": "Let $s\\ge1$ be an integer and let $a=x_0<\\cdots<x_s=b$ be a finite partition, and on each cell $[x_i,x_{i+1}]$ choose an integer degree $n_i\\ge0$ and real controls $c_{i,0},\\ldots,c_{i,n_i}$. For $t=(x-x_i)/(x_{i+1}-x_i)\\in[0,1]$, define the Bernstein basis polynomial\n$$B_{k,n_i}(t)=\\iota\\binom{n_i}{k}t^k(1-t)^{n_i-k},\\qquad 0\\le k\\le n_i,$$\nwhere $\\binom{n_i}{k}$ is the natural binomial coefficient from [[def-binomial-coefficient]], $\\iota$ is the canonical embedding of [[def-canonical-natural]] into $\\mathbb R$, and finite sums use [[def-finite-sum]]. The cell polynomial is $p_i(x)=\\sum_{k=0}^{n_i}c_{i,k}B_{k,n_i}(t)$. These cell polynomials form a piecewise Bernstein representation. To require continuity at a shared node, impose the additional endpoint-control condition $c_{i,n_i}=c_{i+1,0}$; continuity is not part of the representation unless that condition is imposed.\n\nFor degree two this formula reads $p_i(x)=c_{i,0}(1-t)^2+\\iota\\binom21\\,c_{i,1}t(1-t)+c_{i,2}t^2$."
        },
        {
          "id": "thm-piecewise-cubic-hermite-interpolant-properties",
          "kind": "theorem",
          "deps": [
            "def-piecewise-cubic-hermite-interpolant",
            "lem-derivative-algebra-and-chain-rules"
          ],
          "dependency_level": 1,
          "title": "Interpolation, C1 matching, and cubic reproduction for Hermite pieces",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "literature-derived",
            "proof": "ai-generated"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "Carl R. de Boor, A Practical Guide to Splines, revised edition, Chapter IV, pp. 39–40",
                "url": "https://djvu.online/file/W5YE3VGOgt1Ml"
              }
            ]
          },
          "statement": "For the data in [[def-piecewise-cubic-hermite-interpolant]], each piece has the prescribed endpoint values and slopes: $P_i(x_i)=y_i$, $P_i(x_{i+1})=y_{i+1}$, $P_i'(x_i)=m_i$, and $P_i'(x_{i+1})=m_{i+1}$. The resulting interpolant is $C^1$ on $[x_0,x_N]$ (with the corresponding one-sided derivatives at the endpoints). On a single interval, if the four prescribed endpoint data are the values and derivatives of a polynomial $q$ of degree at most three, then $P_i=q$ on that interval."
