{
      "id": "wavelet-transforms-and-nonlinear-approximation",
      "kind": "A",
      "category": "numerical-linear-algebra-and-approximation",
      "label": "APP-WAVELET",
      "title": "Wavelet transforms and nonlinear approximation",
      "companion": "wavelet-transforms-and-nonlinear-approximation-examples",
      "requires": [
        "computed-fast-transforms-and-convolution",
        "polynomial-approximation-and-chebyshev-interpolation"
      ],
      "description": "{\"scope\": [\"Define finite periodic Haar transform first, then orthogonal compact-filter DWT with explicit length/padding/boundary convention; prove analysis/synthesis inverse and Parseval from filter conditions.\", \"Define hard/soft thresholding and best m-term coefficient retention; prove exact l2 discarded-tail error and best m-term optimality for orthonormal basis.\", \"For Haar prove approximation bound for an explicit Sobolev/BV class using cell averages; for higher-order wavelets prove vanishing-moment/local regularity coefficient estimate and Besov-tail rate with exact norm conventions. Biorthogonal transforms require Riesz constants in error bounds.\"], \"proof_route\": \"Finite orthogonal filter-bank algebra; orthogonal projection; cell Poincare and vanishing-moment Taylor cancellation; no use of an unproved general wavelet-basis theorem.\", \"examples\": [\"Haar compression of step function\", \"Boundary extension changes coefficients\", \"Soft threshold bias versus best-m hard threshold\"]}",
      "items": [
        {
          "id": "def-na-finite-periodic-haar-transform",
          "deps": [
            "def-euclidean-inner-product"
          ],
          "dependency_level": 0,
          "kind": "definition",
          "title": "Finite periodic Haar transform",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "literature-derived",
            "proof": "not-applicable"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "Gilbert Strang and Kevin Amaratunga, Wavelets and Filter Banks, MIT OpenCourseWare 18.327, Handouts 3 and 7",
                "url": "https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/18-327-wavelets-filter-banks-and-applications-spring-2003/9ae8fd2b4313aea9cb1d828e3d431301_Handout3.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "Stéphane Mallat, A Wavelet Tour of Signal Processing, 3rd ed., Chapter 9",
                "url": "https://www.di.ens.fr/~mallat/College/WaveletTourChap9.pdf"
              }
            ]
          },
          "statement": "Let $J\\ge1$, $N=2^J$, and $x\\in\\mathbb R^N$, with the Euclidean inner product and norm of [[def-euclidean-inner-product]]. Put $a^{(0)}=x$. For $r=1,\\ldots,J$ and $0\\le k<2^{J-r}$ define\n$$a^{(r)}_k=\\frac{a^{(r-1)}_{2k}+a^{(r-1)}_{2k+1}}{\\sqrt2},\\qquad d^{(r)}_k=\\frac{a^{(r-1)}_{2k}-a^{(r-1)}_{2k+1}}{\\sqrt2}.$$\nThe finite periodic Haar transform $T_Hx$ is the length-$N$ vector obtained by listing $a^{(J)}_0$, then $d^{(J)}_0$, then the entries of $d^{(J-1)}$, and continuing through $d^{(1)}$. The input is already of even dyadic length; this convention uses no padding and no endpoint extension."
        },
        {
          "id": "def-na-periodic-orthogonal-compact-filter-dwt",
          "deps": [
            "def-euclidean-inner-product"
          ],
          "dependency_level": 0,
          "kind": "definition",
          "title": "Periodic finite orthogonal compact-filter wavelet transform",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "literature-derived",
            "proof": "not-applicable"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "Gilbert Strang and Kevin Amaratunga, Wavelets and Filter Banks, MIT OpenCourseWare 18.327, Handouts 3 and 7",
                "url": "https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/18-327-wavelets-filter-banks-and-applications-spring-2003/f95f7897eb0d62fc82ab88c3ae3937e6_Handout7.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "Stéphane Mallat, A Wavelet Tour of Signal Processing, 3rd ed., Chapter 9",
                "url": "https://www.di.ens.fr/~mallat/College/WaveletTourChap9.pdf"
              }
            ]
          },
          "statement": "Let $L\\ge1$, let $N$ be even with $N\\ge2L$, and let $h=(h_0,\\ldots,h_{L-1})\\in\\mathbb R^L$. Set $g_j=(-1)^j h_{L-1-j}$ for $0\\le j<L$, and set $h_j=g_j=0$ for all other integers $j$. The pair $(h,g)$ is an admissible orthogonal filter pair when\n$$\\sum_jh_j=\\sqrt2,\\quad \\sum_jg_j=0,\\quad \\sum_jh_jh_{j-2r}=\\delta_{r0},\\quad \\sum_jg_jg_{j-2r}=\\delta_{r0},\\quad \\sum_jh_jg_{j-2r}=0\\quad(r\\in\\mathbb Z).$$\nHere $\\delta_{r0}=1$ for $r=0$ and $0$ for $r\\ne0$.\nFor $x\\in\\mathbb R^N$, define\n$$a_k=\\sum_{j=0}^{L-1}h_jx_{(2k+j)\\bmod N},\\qquad d_k=\\sum_{j=0}^{L-1}g_jx_{(2k+j)\\bmod N}\\qquad(0\\le k<N/2).$$\nThis is the one-level periodic analysis map. It has no padding: the indices are reduced modulo $N$. Its matrix $W$ has the $N/2$ rows for $a_k$ followed by the $N/2$ rows for $d_k$. Synthesis is, by definition, multiplication by $W^T$. A multilevel transform repeats this one-level rule on the approximation vector while its current length $M$ is even and $M\\ge2L$, then stops; the boundary convention at every level is periodic modulo that level's current length."
        },
        {
          "id": "def-na-daubechies-d4-filter",
          "deps": [],
          "dependency_level": 0,
          "kind": "definition",
          "title": "Explicit four-tap Daubechies filter and its high-pass partner",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "literature-derived",
            "proof": "not-applicable"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "Gilbert Strang and Kevin Amaratunga, Wavelets and Filter Banks, MIT OpenCourseWare 18.327, Handout 8",
                "url": "https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/18-327-wavelets-filter-banks-and-applications-spring-2003/29303d64110d5953075d8f3781ce61fe_Handout8.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "Jelena Kovačević, Vivek K. Goyal, and Martin Vetterli, Fourier and Wavelet Signal Processing",
                "url": "https://www.fourierandwavelets.org/FWSP_a3.2_2013.pdf"
              }
            ]
          },
          "statement": "Set\n$$h=\\frac1{4\\sqrt2}(1+\\sqrt3,\\ 3+\\sqrt3,\\ 3-\\sqrt3,\\ 1-\\sqrt3),\\qquad g_j=(-1)^jh_{3-j}\\quad(0\\le j\\le3).$$\nBoth filters are zero outside the indices $0,1,2,3$. The high-pass filter is therefore $g=(h_3,-h_2,h_1,-h_0)$. The exact admissibility and two-moment properties of this pair are the statement of [[thm-na-daubechies-d4-filter-qmf-and-two-moments]].",
          "justified_by": [
            "thm-na-daubechies-d4-filter-qmf-and-two-moments"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "def-na-smooth-derivative-bound-class",
          "deps": [
            "def-continuity-and-derivative-on-real-interval"
          ],
          "dependency_level": 0,
          "kind": "definition",
          "title": "Smooth functions on the unit interval with bounded derivative data",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "literature-derived",
            "proof": "not-applicable"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "Gilbert Strang and Kevin Amaratunga, Wavelets and Filter Banks, MIT OpenCourseWare 18.327, Handout 13",
                "url": "https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/18-327-wavelets-filter-banks-and-applications-spring-2003/99231bc0f90b87508c01a2e7f5a960e7_Handout13.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "Stéphane Mallat, A Wavelet Tour of Signal Processing, 3rd ed., Chapter 9",
                "url": "https://www.di.ens.fr/~mallat/College/WaveletTourChap9.pdf"
              }
            ]
          },
          "statement": "Let $q\\ge1$ be an integer and let $U\\subseteq\\mathbb R$ be an open interval containing $[0,1]$. A real function $f:U\\to\\mathbb R$ belongs to the smooth derivative-bound class $\\mathcal S^q$ when it is $C^q$ on $U$ in the sense of [[def-continuity-and-derivative-on-real-interval]]. For $0\\le r\\le q$ define $M_r(f)=\\sup_{0\\le t\\le1}|f^{(r)}(t)|$, with $f^{(0)}=f$. These derivative bounds are finite because the derivatives are continuous on the compact interval. They are bounds on the restriction to $[0,1]$, not a norm on arbitrary extensions to $U$; no claim that this class exhausts $W^{q,\\infty}(0,1)$ is part of the definition."
        },
        {
          "id": "def-na-haar-cell-average-projection",
          "deps": [],
          "dependency_level": 0,
          "kind": "definition",
          "title": "Haar cell-average projection on the unit interval",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "literature-derived",
            "proof": "not-applicable"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "Gilbert Strang and Kevin Amaratunga, Wavelets and Filter Banks, MIT OpenCourseWare 18.327, Handout 3",
                "url": "https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/18-327-wavelets-filter-banks-and-applications-spring-2003/9ae8fd2b4313aea9cb1d828e3d431301_Handout3.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "Stéphane Mallat, A Wavelet Tour of Signal Processing, 3rd ed., Chapter 9",
                "url": "https://www.di.ens.fr/~mallat/College/WaveletTourChap9.pdf"
              }
            ]
          },
          "statement": "For an integer $J\\ge0$, put $h=2^{-J}$ and $I_{J,k}=[kh,(k+1)h]$ for $0\\le k<2^J$. For an integrable real function $f$ on $[0,1]$, its Haar cell-average projection is the piecewise constant function\n$$P_Jf(x)=\\frac1h\\int_{I_{J,k}}f(t)\\,dt\\quad\\text{when }x\\in[kh,(k+1)h).$$\nThe value at $x=1$ is defined by the average on the last cell. The half-open intervals give an unambiguous partition for point values; changing endpoint values does not change the integrals or the $L^2$ error."
        },
        {
          "id": "def-na-hard-soft-coefficient-thresholding",
          "deps": [
            "def-euclidean-inner-product"
          ],
          "dependency_level": 0,
          "kind": "definition",
          "title": "Hard m-term retention and soft thresholding of finite coefficients",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "literature-derived",
            "proof": "not-applicable"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
