      "description": "{\"scope\": [\"Define type1 F_k=sum_j c_j exp(-ik x_j), type2 values sum_k f_k exp(ik x_j), and type3 nonuniform frequencies; domains, integer mode convention and coordinate wrapping are fixed.\", \"Define oversampled gridding/spreading, periodic kernel, width w, deconvolution factors, FFT and interpolation; return failure when denominator enclosure contains0 or truncation/range criterion fails.\", \"Prove exact infinite-kernel Poisson/aliasing identity then finite-width approximation bound ||Fhat-F||_infty <= eps ||c||_1 + rounding_bound with eps from explicitly proved kernel Fourier/tail estimates; type2 is adjoint under matching scaling. Complexity O(M log M+N w^d) needs fixed d and bounded oversampling; type3 includes frequency-space scale/product and cannot inherit type1 cost blindly.\"], \"proof_route\": \"Poisson summation under explicitly owned absolute convergence hypotheses; derive kernel tail and alias sum, propagate deconvolution lower bound and FFT error.\", \"examples\": [\"Nonuniform MRI Fourier samples\", \"Clustered points and l1 absolute error\", \"Near-zero mode deconvolution failure\"]}",
      "items": [
        {
          "id": "def-nufft-finite-array-norms",
          "kind": "definition",
          "title": "Finite complex-array norms used by the NUFFT certificates",
          "statement": "For a finite index set $E$ and a complex array $z=(z_e)_{e\\in E}$, define\n$$\\|z\\|_1=\\sum_{e\\in E}|z_e|,\\qquad \\|z\\|_2=\\left(\\sum_{e\\in E}|z_e|^2\\right)^{1/2},\\qquad \\|z\\|_\\infty=\\max_{e\\in E}|z_e|.$$\nFor $z,w\\in\\mathbb C^E$, define the counting inner product by $\\langle z,w\\rangle_E=\\sum_{e\\in E}z_e\\overline{w_e}$. By [[lem-complex-conjugation-and-modulus-laws]], $\\langle z,z\\rangle_E=\\sum_{e\\in E}|z_e|^2$, so $\\|z\\|_2=\\sqrt{\\langle z,z\\rangle_E}$. For $E=\\varnothing$, all three norms and this inner product are defined to be zero. For nonempty finite $E$, the maximum exists by repeated comparison in the real order. Complex modulus is as in [[def-intervals-rectangles-and-complex-balls]], conjugation as in [[def-complex-conjugate-real-imaginary-part-and-modulus]], finite sums as in [[def-finite-sum-in-a-commutative-monoid]], and the square root is the unique nonnegative root.",
          "deps": [
            "def-finite-sum-in-a-commutative-monoid",
            "def-intervals-rectangles-and-complex-balls",
            "def-complex-conjugate-real-imaginary-part-and-modulus",
            "lem-complex-conjugation-and-modulus-laws",
            "lem-nonnegative-square-root-exists-and-unique",
            "def-complete-ordered-real-field",
            "thm-induction-principle"
          ],
          "dependency_level": 0,
          "strategy": "Give explicit finite-sum/max formulas and state the empty-set convention.",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "Sheldon Axler, Linear Algebra Done Right, fourth edition",
                "url": "https://linear.axler.net/LADR4e.pdf",
                "locator": "Section 6A, printed pp. 182–186: complex Euclidean norm and counting inner product, Example 6.3(a), Definition 6.7 and Example 6.8(a). The local finite-index/empty-index, l1 and maximum conventions and finite well-definedness proof are supplied explicitly here."
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "def-nufft-periodized-gaussian-gridding-kernel",
          "kind": "definition",
          "title": "Periodized Gaussian gridding kernel",
          "statement": "For a real parameter $\\tau>0$, define the period-one Gaussian gridding kernel by the series\n\n$$\\phi_\\tau(x)=\\sum_{n\\in\\mathbb Z}\\exp\\!\\left(-\\frac{\\pi(x+n)^2}{\\tau}\\right),\\qquad x\\in\\mathbb R.$$\n\nThe series is the formal periodization; [[lem-nufft-gaussian-periodization-converges]] proves that it converges absolutely and locally uniformly, so its value is a continuous one-periodic real function. Its Fourier coefficient at $r\\in\\mathbb Z$ is denoted $\\widehat\\phi_\\tau(r)=\\int_0^1\\phi_\\tau(x)e^{-2\\pi i r x}\\,dx$ when this integral is used; [[lem-nufft-gaussian-periodization-fourier-series]] computes it exactly. The kernel is nonnegative and satisfies $\\phi_\\tau(x+1)=\\phi_\\tau(x)$.",
          "deps": [
            "def-real-elementary-functions-and-domains",
            "cor-exponential-reciprocal-and-positivity"
          ],
          "dependency_level": 0,
          "justified_by": [
