          "url": "https://www.math.ucdavis.edu/~saito/data/tensor/lathauwer-etal_mulilinear-SVD.pdf"
        },
        {
          "title": "ACM 270-1, Complete Lecture Notes, Caltech, Spring 2019, Lecture 14, §3",
          "url": "https://iqim.caltech.edu/wp-content/uploads/2019/08/ACM270_complete_lecture_notes.pdf"
        }
      ]
    }
  },
  {
    "id": "def-tensor-train-cores-bond-ranks-and-canonical-gauges",
    "kind": "definition",
    "title": "Tensor train cores, bond ranks, and canonical gauges",
    "statement": "Let $A\\in\\mathbb F^{n_1\\times\\cdots\\times n_d}$ with $d\\ge2$. A tensor-train (TT) representation consists of integers $r_0=r_d=1$, internal bond sizes $r_1,\\ldots,r_{d-1}\\in\\mathbb N_0$, and cores\n$$ G_k\\in\\mathbb F^{r_{k-1}\\times n_k\\times r_k}\\quad(1\\le k\\le d) $$\nsuch that\n$$ A_{i_1,\\ldots,i_d} =\\sum_{\\alpha_1=1}^{r_1}\\cdots\\sum_{\\alpha_{d-1}=1}^{r_{d-1}} G_1(1,i_1,\\alpha_1)G_2(\\alpha_1,i_2,\\alpha_2)\\cdots G_d(\\alpha_{d-1},i_d,1). $$\nAn empty bond index set gives an empty sum, hence the zero tensor; in particular, all internal bond sizes zero represent zero. A bond size $r_k$ is the storage dimension contracted between cores $k$ and $k+1$. The cut-$k$ unfolding groups modes $1,\\ldots,k$ against $k+1,\\ldots,d$ as fixed in [[def-finite-tensor-arrays-and-frobenius-inner-product]].\n\nFor an internal bond $k$, an invertible matrix $S\\in\\mathbb F^{r_k\\times r_k}$ gives a gauge transformation by replacing the two adjacent cores with\n$$ \\widetilde G_k(\\alpha,i,\\beta)=\\sum_{\\gamma=1}^{r_k}G_k(\\alpha,i,\\gamma)S_{\\gamma\\beta}, \\qquad \\widetilde G_{k+1}(\\beta,j,\\eta)=\\sum_{\\gamma=1}^{r_k}(S^{-1})_{\\beta\\gamma}G_{k+1}(\\gamma,j,\\eta). $$\nTheir contraction is unchanged because $\\sum_\\beta S_{\\gamma\\beta}(S^{-1})_{\\beta\\gamma'}=\\delta_{\\gamma,\\gamma'}$.\n\nThe left matricization of core $G_k$ has row index $(\\alpha_{k-1},i_k)$ and column index $\\alpha_k$. The core is left-isometric if this matrix $L_k$ satisfies $L_k^*L_k=I_{r_k}$. The right matricization has row index $\\alpha_{k-1}$ and column index $(i_k,\\alpha_k)$; the core is right-isometric if its matrix $R_k$ satisfies $R_kR_k^*=I_{r_{k-1}}$. A TT is in mixed canonical gauge about core $\\ell$ when cores $k<\\ell$ are left-isometric and cores $k>\\ell$ are right-isometric.",
    "deps": [
      "def-finite-tensor-arrays-and-frobenius-inner-product"
    ],
    "dependency_level": 1,
    "provenance": {
      "statement": "literature-derived",
      "proof": "not-applicable"
    },
    "sources": {
      "references": [
        {
          "title": "Ivan V. Oseledets, Tensor-Train Decomposition, SIAM Journal on Scientific Computing 33(5) (2011), §§2–4",
          "url": "https://users.math.msu.edu/users/iwenmark/Teaching/CMSE890/TENSOR_oseledets2011.pdf"
        },
        {
          "title": "Mi-Song Dupuy, Tensor trains for high-dimensional problems, full lecture notes, Chapter 1, §§1.2.1–1.2.5 (2026)",
          "url": "https://msdupuy.github.io/teaching/TT_lectures_2026.pdf"
        }
      ]
    }
  },
  {
    "id": "def-canonical-polyadic-rank",
    "kind": "definition",
    "title": "Canonical polyadic rank",
    "statement": "For $A\\in\\mathbb F^{n_1\\times\\cdots\\times n_d}$, its canonical polyadic rank (CP rank) is the least integer $R\\ge0$ for which\n$$ A=\\sum_{q=1}^{R}a_q^{(1)}\\otimes\\cdots\\otimes a_q^{(d)},\\qquad a_q^{(k)}\\in\\mathbb F^{n_k}. $$\nThe empty sum is zero, so the zero tensor has CP rank $0$. The coordinate expansion\n$$ A=\\sum_{i_1=1}^{n_1}\\cdots\\sum_{i_d=1}^{n_d} A_{i_1,\\ldots,i_d}\\,e_{i_1}^{(1)}\\otimes\\cdots\\otimes e_{i_d}^{(d)} $$\nshows that the set of admissible finite integers $R$ is nonempty. The well-ordering of $\\mathbb N_0$ therefore makes the least such $R$ well-defined.",
    "deps": [
      "def-finite-tensor-arrays-and-frobenius-inner-product"
    ],
    "dependency_level": 1,
    "provenance": {
      "statement": "literature-derived",
      "proof": "not-applicable"
    },
    "sources": {
      "references": [
        {
          "title": "Tamara G. Kolda and Brett W. Bader, Tensor Decompositions and Applications, SIAM Review 51(3) (2009), §3",
          "url": "https://www.kolda.net/publication/TensorReview.pdf"
        },
        {
          "title": "ACM 270-1, Complete Lecture Notes, Caltech, Spring 2019, Lecture 13, §4.2",
          "url": "https://iqim.caltech.edu/wp-content/uploads/2019/08/ACM270_complete_lecture_notes.pdf"
        }
      ]
    }
  },
  {
    "id": "thm-best-rank-k-frobenius-matrix-approximation",
    "kind": "theorem",
    "title": "Best rank-k matrix approximation in Frobenius norm",
    "statement": "Let $A\\in\\mathbb F^{m\\times n}$, where $m,n\\ge1$, have a singular value decomposition\n$$ A=\\sum_{j=1}^{q}\\sigma_j u_jv_j^*,\\qquad \\sigma_1\\ge\\cdots\\ge\\sigma_q>0, $$\nwhere $q=\\operatorname{rank}(A)$. For an integer $k\\ge0$, put\n$$ A_k=\\sum_{j=1}^{\\min(k,q)}\\sigma_j u_jv_j^*, \\qquad \\delta_k^2=\\sum_{j=k+1}^{q}\\sigma_j^2 $$\nwith an empty sum equal to zero. Then $\\operatorname{rank}(A_k)\\le k$, $\\|A-A_k\\|_F=\\delta_k$, and every matrix $B$ with $\\operatorname{rank}(B)\\le k$ satisfies $\\|A-B\\|_F\\ge\\delta_k$.",
    "deps": [
      "def-finite-tensor-arrays-and-frobenius-inner-product",
      "thm-singular-value-decomposition"
    ],
    "dependency_level": 1,
