    "id": "def-ewald-causal-cardinal-spline-window",
    "kind": "definition",
    "title": "Causal cardinal spline window",
    "deps": [
      "def-convolution-of-two-functions-on-rn"
    ],
    "dependency_level": 0,
    "strategy": "Define the causal order-P cardinal window by its finite truncated-power formula so periodic assignment is pointwise and finite.",
    "justified_by": [],
    "provenance": {
      "statement": "literature-derived",
      "proof": "not-applicable"
    },
    "sources": {
      "references": [
        {
          "title": "Markus Deserno and Christian Holm, How to mesh up Ewald sums (I): A theoretical and numerical comparison of various particle mesh routines (1998)",
          "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/cond-mat/9807099",
          "locator": "§3, equations (19)–(20), and Appendix E: order-P cardinal spline as the P-fold convolution of the unit-interval indicator and its tensor-product use."
        },
        {
          "title": "Multi-Level Ewald: A hybrid multigrid / Fast Fourier Transform approach to the electrostatic particle-mesh problem (2011)",
          "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3202610/",
          "locator": "§2.1, equations (2)–(7): cardinal B-spline charge assignment and particle-to-mesh interpolation."
        }
      ]
    },
    "statement": "For $P=1$ set $W_1(u)=\\mathbf 1_{[0,1]}(u)$, including both endpoints. For an\ninteger $P\\ge2$, define the causal order-$P$ window by\n$$W_P(u)=\\begin{cases}\\displaystyle\\frac1{(P-1)!}\\sum_{j=0}^{P}(-1)^j\\binom Pj(u-j)_+^{P-1},&0\\le u\\le P,\\\\0,&u<0\\text{ or }u>P.\\end{cases}$$\nHere $s_+=\\max(s,0)$, $m!$ is the factorial of the nonnegative integer $m$\nwith $0!=1$, and for integers $0\\le j\\le P$,\n$\\binom Pj=P!/(j!(P-j)!)$. Convolution means the integral operation\n$$(f*g)(u)=\\int_{\\mathbb R}f(u-s)g(s)\\,ds,$$\nwhen the integrand is measurable and integrable, as in\n[[def-convolution-of-two-functions-on-rn]]. The displayed formula is finite at\nevery $u$ and directly defines $W_P$ to vanish outside $[0,P]$; the local\nalias lemma proves that it agrees with the $P$-fold convolution of $W_1$ and\nestablishes the resulting integral, positivity, partition, Fourier-transform,\nand derivative identities."
  },
  {
    "id": "def-ewald-three-dimensional-periodic-source-data",
    "kind": "definition",
    "title": "Neutral point sources and Fourier data on a three-dimensional torus",
    "deps": [
      "def-full-rank-lattice-covolume-and-dual-lattice"
    ],
    "dependency_level": 0,
    "strategy": "Fix the physical and reciprocal lattices, torus coordinates, Fourier convention, and neutral finite source data.",
    "justified_by": [],
    "provenance": {
      "statement": "ai-altered",
      "proof": "not-applicable"
    },
    "sources": {
      "references": [
        {
          "title": "Hark Lee and Wei Cai, Ewald Summation for Coulomb Interactions in a Periodic Supercell (2009 lecture notes)",
          "url": "https://warwick.ac.uk/fac/sci/moac/degreescopy/degrees-copy/modules/ch926/lectures/molsim/ewaldsummationnotes.pdf",
          "locator": "§1, equations (1)–(7), printed pp. 1–2: periodic images, the cell, pairwise-distinct sources, and the point-source interaction convention."
        },
        {
          "title": "Cameron F. Abrams, Modern Molecular Simulations (CHE T580, 2021 lecture notes)",
          "url": "https://research.coe.drexel.edu/cbe/abramsgroup/msim2/CHE_T580_Lecture_Notes.pdf",
          "locator": "Chapter 7, §7 Long-Range Interactions: The Ewald Summation, printed pp. 85–87: periodic point charges, reciprocal frequencies, neutrality, and the zero mode."
        }
      ]
    },
    "statement": "Let $A\\in GL_3(\\mathbb R)$, let $\\Lambda=A\\mathbb Z^3$, let\n$V=|\\det A|$, and let $\\mathbb T_A=\\mathbb R^3/\\Lambda$. These are the\nfull-rank lattice and covolume conventions of\n[[def-full-rank-lattice-covolume-and-dual-lattice]]. The reciprocal lattice is\n$\\Lambda^*=A^{-T}\\mathbb Z^3$. For $k\\in\\mathbb Z^3$ write\n$$\\kappa_k=2\\pi A^{-T}k,\\qquad e_k(x)=e^{i\\kappa_k\\cdot x}.$$\nThus $e_k$ is a character of $\\mathbb T_A$; the ordinary Fourier frequency is\n$A^{-T}k$, while $\\kappa_k$ is the angular physical frequency.\n\nA finite point-source configuration consists of an integer $n\\ge0$, points\n$x_1,\\ldots,x_n\\in\\mathbb T_A$ that are pairwise distinct when $n>1$, and\nreal charges $q_1,\\ldots,q_n$. It is **neutral** when\n$\\sum_{i=1}^n q_i=0$. For a smooth periodic test function $\\varphi$, the\npoint-source distribution is the linear functional\n$$\\langle\\rho,\\varphi\\rangle=\\sum_{i=1}^nq_i\\varphi(x_i).$$\nHere smooth means that the periodic lift to $\\mathbb R^3$ has continuous\nderivatives of every order, and the test-function topology is generated by the\nseminorms $\\max_{x\\in A[0,1]^3}|\\partial^\\beta\\varphi(x)|$ over all derivative\nmulti-indices $\\beta$. A periodic distribution is a continuous complex-linear\nfunctional for this topology. Its distributional Laplacian is defined by\n$\\langle-\\Delta u,\\varphi\\rangle=\\langle u,-\\Delta\\varphi\\rangle$.\n\nFor a smooth periodic $\\varphi$, its Fourier coefficient is\n$$\\widehat\\varphi(k)=\\frac1V\\int_{A(0,1]^3}\\varphi(x)e^{-i\\kappa_k\\cdot x}\\,dx.$$\nThe source coefficient is\n$$\\widehat\\rho(k)=\\frac1V\\sum_{i=1}^nq_i e^{-i\\kappa_k\\cdot x_i};$$\nin particular, neutral data have $\\widehat\\rho(0)=0$. For a periodic\ndistribution $u$, define $\\widehat u(k)=V^{-1}\\langle u,e_{-k}\\rangle$. The\n**mean-zero periodic Poisson solution** is a periodic distribution $u$ satisfying\n$-\\Delta u=4\\pi\\rho$ and $\\widehat u(0)=0$. This definition fixes the\nzero-mode convention; no nonneutral periodic solution is implicit.\n\nThe Coulomb energy convention is the half-sum of each source charge times the\nregular part of its potential, after subtracting its own singular term\n$q_i/|x-x_i|$. The resulting finite part is called the **renormalized\nself-energy convention**. Its Ewald formula is established in this development."
  },
  {
    "id": "lem-ewald-screened-coulomb-kernel-and-self-limit",
    "kind": "lemma",
    "title": "Gaussian screened Coulomb kernel and its self limit",
    "deps": [
      "cor-c-one-change-of-variables-for-l-one-functions",
      "def-countable-choice",
      "thm-chain-rule",
      "thm-divergence-theorem-for-bounded-c-one-euclidean-domains",
      "thm-ftc-first-part",
      "thm-fubini-theorem-for-l-one-on-sigma-finite-product-spaces",
      "thm-gaussian-integral"
    ],
    "dependency_level": 0,
    "strategy": "Differentiate the radial error-function potential, verify its distributional source by the flux at the origin, and compute the finite self part.",
    "justified_by": [],
    "provenance": {
      "statement": "literature-derived",
      "proof": "ai-altered"
    },
    "sources": {
      "references": [
        {
          "title": "Hark Lee and Wei Cai, Ewald Summation for Coulomb Interactions in a Periodic Supercell (2009 lecture notes)",
          "url": "https://warwick.ac.uk/fac/sci/moac/degreescopy/degrees-copy/modules/ch926/lectures/molsim/ewaldsummationnotes.pdf",
          "locator": "§§3–4, equations (13)–(25), printed pp. 3–5: Gaussian screening, erf/erfc radial potentials, and the self limit."
        },
        {
          "title": "Cameron F. Abrams, Modern Molecular Simulations (CHE T580, 2021 lecture notes)",
          "url": "https://research.coe.drexel.edu/cbe/abramsgroup/msim2/CHE_T580_Lecture_Notes.pdf",
          "locator": "Chapter 7, equations (242)–(248), printed pp. 86–87: the Gaussian potential, its self value, and the screened real-space term."
        }
      ]
    },
    "statement": "Assume Countable Choice. For $\\alpha>0$, define\n$$g_\\alpha(x)=\\frac{\\alpha^3}{\\pi^{3/2}}e^{-\\alpha^2|x|^2},\\quad \\operatorname{erf}(s)=\\frac2{\\sqrt\\pi}\\int_0^s e^{-t^2}\\,dt,\\quad \\operatorname{erfc}(s)=1-\\operatorname{erf}(s).$$\nHere $\\Delta=\\sum_{j=1}^3\\partial_{x_j}^2$, $\\delta_0$ is the distribution\n$\\langle\\delta_0,\\varphi\\rangle=\\varphi(0)$, and a test function is a smooth\ncompactly supported function $\\varphi$.\nFor $r=|x|>0$ put $\\phi_\\ell(r)=\\operatorname{erf}(\\alpha r)/r$ and\n$\\phi_s(r)=\\operatorname{erfc}(\\alpha r)/r$. Then $\\phi_\\ell$ extends\ncontinuously to $r=0$, $-\\Delta\\phi_\\ell=4\\pi g_\\alpha$ distributionally on\n$\\mathbb R^3$, and $-\\Delta\\phi_s=4\\pi\\delta_0-4\\pi g_\\alpha$. Moreover,\nfor every $s\\ge0$,\n$$\\operatorname{erfc}(s)=\\frac2{\\sqrt\\pi}\\int_s^\\infty e^{-t^2}\\,dt,$$\n$$\\phi_s(r)=\\frac1r-\\frac{2\\alpha}{\\sqrt\\pi}+O(r^2)\\quad(r\\downarrow0),$$\nand for every $r>0$,\n$$0\\le\\phi_s(r)\\le\\frac{e^{-\\alpha^2r^2}}{\\sqrt\\pi\\alpha r^2},\\qquad |\\phi_s'(r)|\\le\\frac{2\\alpha}{\\sqrt\\pi}\\frac{e^{-\\alpha^2r^2}}r+ \\frac{e^{-\\alpha^2r^2}}{\\sqrt\\pi\\alpha r^3}.$$"
  },
  {
    "id": "def-ewald-pme-cardinal-assignment-model",
    "kind": "definition",
    "title": "Periodic cardinal-spline assignment and unitary mesh transform",
    "deps": [
      "def-ewald-causal-cardinal-spline-window",
      "def-ewald-three-dimensional-periodic-source-data",
      "def-unitary-discrete-fourier-transform-on-z-mod-n"
    ],
    "dependency_level": 1,
    "strategy": "Define the causal order-P periodic window, residue accumulation, signed retained modes, denominator guard, and exact output convention.",
    "justified_by": [],
    "provenance": {
      "statement": "literature-derived",
      "proof": "not-applicable"
    },
    "sources": {
      "references": [
        {
          "title": "Markus Deserno and Christian Holm, How to mesh up Ewald sums (I): A theoretical and numerical comparison of various particle mesh routines (1998)",
          "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/cond-mat/9807099",
          "locator": "§3 and Appendix E: cardinal-spline charge assignment, finite mesh modes, Fourier assignment convention, and interpolation support."
        },
        {
          "title": "Multi-Level Ewald: A hybrid multigrid / Fast Fourier Transform approach to the electrostatic particle-mesh problem (full-text article, 2011)",
          "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3202610/",
          "locator": "§2.1, equations (1)–(7): spline assignment, mesh Fourier transform, reciprocal convolution, and force interpolation."
        }
      ]
    },
    "statement": "Fix neutral point-source data $(A,V,\\Lambda,\\kappa_k,(x_i,q_i)_{i=1}^n)$ from\n[[def-ewald-three-dimensional-periodic-source-data]], an Ewald parameter\n$\\alpha>0$, integers $M_1,M_2,M_3\\ge1$, an assignment order $P\\ge2$, and write\n$N_g=M_1M_2M_3$. Let $\\mathcal G=\\prod_{r=1}^3\\{0,\\ldots,M_r-1\\}$ and take\n$W_P$ from [[def-ewald-causal-cardinal-spline-window]]. Define the periodic\ncausal assignment weights, for $t\\in[0,1)^3$ and $\\ell\\in\\mathcal G$, by\n$$w_\\ell(t)=\\prod_{r=1}^3\\sum_{j\\in\\mathbb Z} W_P(M_rt_r-\\ell_r-jM_r).$$\nThe sums are finite because $W_P$ is supported on $[0,P]$. They collect every\nsupport index into its grid residue; if $P>M_r$, distinct support indices that\nwrap to the same residue are added. The spread grid is\n$$g_\\ell=\\sum_{i=1}^n q_iw_\\ell(t_i),\\qquad t_i=A^{-1}x_i\\pmod{\\mathbb Z^3}.$$\n\nUse the negative-sign unitary tensor DFT convention\n$$\\widehat g_M(k)=N_g^{-1/2}\\sum_{\\ell\\in\\mathcal G}g_\\ell e^{-2\\pi i\\sum_{r=1}^3k_r\\ell_r/M_r},$$\nwhich is the product transform of\n[[def-unitary-discrete-fourier-transform-on-z-mod-n]]. Signed frequency\nrepresentatives are chosen before evaluating windows. A retained set\n$\\mathcal K\\subset\\mathbb Z^3\\setminus\\{0\\}$ is finite, symmetric under\n$k\\mapsto-k$, injective modulo $(M_1,M_2,M_3)$, and satisfies\n$|k_r|<M_r/2$ for each coordinate. In particular, even-grid Nyquist modes are\nnot silently retained.\n\nFor $u\\in\\mathbb R$, put $\\operatorname{sinc}(u)=\\sin(u)/u$ when $u\\ne0$ and\n$\\operatorname{sinc}(0)=1$. Define\n$$D(k)=\\prod_{r=1}^3e^{-\\pi iPk_r/M_r} \\operatorname{sinc}(\\pi k_r/M_r)^P.$$\nOnly modes with $D(k)\\ne0$ are admissible for division. A computed\nimplementation must first certify a strictly positive lower bound for\n$|D(k)|$; otherwise it returns `denominator-inconclusive` without dividing.\nFor $k\\in\\mathcal K$, define\n$$a_k(t)=e^{-2\\pi i k\\cdot t},\\quad b_k(t)=\\frac{\\sum_{\\ell\\in\\mathcal G}w_\\ell(t) e^{-2\\pi i\\sum_rk_r\\ell_r/M_r}}{\\overline{D(k)}},\\quad \\rho_k=\\sum_iq_i a_k(t_i),\\quad z_k=\\frac{\\sqrt{N_g}\\,\\widehat g_M(k)}{\\overline{D(k)}}.$$\nThe denominator is the complex conjugate of the printed window multiplier; this\nsign is part of the definition. Set\n$$c_k=\\frac{4\\pi}{V|\\kappa_k|^2} e^{-|\\kappa_k|^2/(4\\alpha^2)}.$$\nThe exact retained reciprocal energy is\n$$E_{\\mathcal K}=\\frac12\\sum_{k\\in\\mathcal K}c_k|\\rho_k|^2,$$\nand the mesh reciprocal energy is\n$$E_M=\\frac12\\sum_{k\\in\\mathcal K}c_k|z_k|^2.$$\n\nLet $\\mathfrak d_k(t)$ be the declared physical mesh derivative: it is\n$\\nabla_xb_k(t)=A^{-T}\\nabla_tb_k(t)$ wherever the classical derivative\nexists. A grid knot is a point with $M_rt_r\\in\\mathbb Z$ for some coordinate\n$r$. At a $P=2$ knot, take the one-sided vector limits from all adjacent open\nmesh boxes. Define $\\mathfrak d_k(t)$ to be any selected one-sided limit or the\narithmetic average of all those limits. This is a procedure value and does not\nassert that the classical derivative exists there. The\nenergy-gradient mesh force is\n$$F_{M,i}=-q_i\\sum_{k\\in\\mathcal K}c_k \\operatorname{Re}\\!\\left(\\overline{z_k}\\mathfrak d_k(t_i)\\right).$$\nDefine the diagonal mesh-self correction and corrected reciprocal energy by\n$$C_M=\\frac12\\sum_iq_i^2\\sum_{k\\in\\mathcal K}c_k (|b_k(t_i)|^2-1),\\qquad E_M^{\\mathrm{corr}}=E_M-C_M.$$\nWhether the declared force is the derivative of this corrected energy, and the\nerror bound it satisfies, are proved separately."
  },
