rescaled variable is at most $2\\delta\\|H\\|_\\infty=4\\|H\\|_\\infty/\\sqrt\\Lambda$ by [F4]. [given, F3, F4, F5, construct, algebra]\n\n2.1 On $[\\delta,1]$, [F1] and [F2] integrate by parts using $(i\\sigma\\Lambda t)^{-1}$ and $\\widetilde H$; the boundary at $1$ vanishes because $\\widetilde H$ is compactly supported in $(-1,1)$. The remaining boundary is at most $\\|H\\|_\\infty/(\\Lambda\\delta)$. The integral term is at most $\\frac1\\Lambda\\left(R\\|H'\\|_\\infty\\int_\\delta^1t^{-1}dt+\\|H\\|_\\infty\\int_\\delta^1t^{-2}dt\\right)\\le\\frac{R\\|H'\\|_\\infty+\\|H\\|_\\infty}{\\Lambda\\delta}$, since both integrals are at most $1/\\delta$. [F4] justifies the complex integral norm bounds; the negative interval has the same estimates. With $\\delta=2\\Lambda^{-1/2}$, each boundary is at most $\\|H\\|_\\infty/(2\\sqrt\\Lambda)$ and each integral is at most $(R\\|H'\\|_\\infty+\\|H\\|_\\infty)/(2\\sqrt\\Lambda)$. By [F5], adding the central piece and two tails gives $(6\\|H\\|_\\infty+R\\|H'\\|_\\infty)/\\sqrt\\Lambda$ in the rescaled integral. Multiplication by $R$ and $\\Lambda=\\lambda R^2$ give the claimed bound. [given, F1, F2, F4, F5, step 1.1, algebra] ∎",
