        {
          "id": "def-distinct-node-divided-difference",
          "kind": "definition",
          "title": "Divided differences on distinct nodes",
          "deps": [],
          "dependency_level": 0,
          "statement": "Let $q\\ge 0$, let $t_0,\\ldots,t_q$ be distinct real numbers, and let $f$ be\nreal-valued at these nodes. Define the **divided difference** recursively by\n$f[t_0]:=f(t_0)$ and, for $q\\ge1$,\n$$f[t_0,\\ldots,t_q]:= \\frac{f[t_1,\\ldots,t_q]-f[t_0,\\ldots,t_{q-1}]}{t_q-t_0}.$$\nAll sublists in the recursion remain distinct. The denominator is nonzero\nbecause $t_q\\ne t_0$, so this recursion gives a finite real number from the\nnode data.",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "literature-derived",
            "proof": "not-applicable"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "Lloyd N. Trefethen, Approximation Theory and Approximation Practice, Chapter 5",
