        {
          "id": "def-floater-hormann-interpolant",
          "kind": "definition",
          "title": "Floater–Hormann barycentric interpolant",
          "deps": [
            "def-barycentric-rational-quotient"
          ],
          "dependency_level": 2,
          "statement": "Let $n\\ge1$, let $x_0<\\cdots<x_n$ be distinct real nodes, let\n$f_0,\\ldots,f_n\\in\\mathbb R$ be data, and let $d$ be an integer with\n$0\\le d<n$. For $j=0,\\ldots,n$, define\n$$w_j=(-1)^{j-d} \\sum_{i=\\max(0,j-d)}^{\\min(j,n-d)} \\prod_{\\substack{i\\le k\\le i+d\\\\k\\ne j}}|x_j-x_k|^{-1}.$$\nThe index range is nonempty and every product is positive, so every $w_j$ is\nnonzero. The **Floater–Hormann interpolant of degree parameter $d$** is the\nbarycentric rational quotient of these nodes, data, and weights from\n[[def-barycentric-rational-quotient]]. Away from the nodes, exact evaluation\nuses the quotient with this denominator;\na zero of the reduced denominator is reported as pole status. The no-real-pole\ntheorem below proves that this status never occurs for admissible exact real\ninputs.",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "literature-derived",
            "proof": "not-applicable"
          },
          "sources": {
