  {
    "id": "def-barycentric-point-location-interpolation-and-voronoi-query",
    "kind": "definition",
    "title": "Barycentric point location, affine interpolation, and nearest-site queries",
    "deps": [
      "def-indexed-geometric-site-set",
      "def-planar-delaunay-voronoi-structures"
    ],
    "justified_by": [
      "thm-barycentric-interpolation-and-nearest-neighbor-query-correct"
    ],
    "dependency_level": 3,
    "statement": "Let $T$ be a nondegenerate triangle with ordered vertices $p_0,p_1,p_2\\in\\mathbb D^2$. For $x\\in\\mathbb R^2$, its **barycentric coordinates** are the unique scalars $\\lambda_0,\\lambda_1,\\lambda_2$ satisfying $\\sum_i\\lambda_i=1$ and $x=\\sum_i\\lambda_i p_i$. They are exact for dyadic $x$: they are obtained by solving the invertible $2\\times2$ system with columns $p_1-p_0,p_2-p_0$. The point is in $T$ exactly when all $\\lambda_i\\ge0$.\n\nGiven vertex data $f_0,f_1,f_2\\in\\mathbb R$, the **barycentric affine interpolant** on $T$ is $I_Tf(x)=\\sum_i\\lambda_i f_i$. A point-location result is a containing triangle of the triangulation, with a shared-edge or shared-vertex result allowed when the point lies on a face; outside the convex hull its status is `outside-hull` and no containing triangle is returned.\n\nFor a nonempty finite unweighted indexed site set $P$, the **nearest-site query** returns a site minimizing $\\|x-p\\|^2$, breaking ties by the least stable identifier. Its hull status is `inside-hull` for $x\\in\\operatorname{conv}P$ and `outside-hull` otherwise. The minimizer is returned in either case.",
    "source_path": "items/def-barycentric-point-location-interpolation-and-voronoi-query.md",
    "provenance": {
      "statement": "literature-derived",
      "proof": "not-applicable"
    },
    "sources": {
      "references": [
        {
          "title": "Mark de Berg et al., Computational Geometry: Algorithms and Applications, 3rd ed., Chapter 9",
          "url": "https://ics-websites.science.uu.nl/geobook/interpolation.pdf"
        },
