  {
    "id": "def-hlr-and-h2-block-matrix-representations",
    "kind": "definition",
    "title": "H-matrix block representation",
    "statement": "Let $\\mathbb F\\in\\{\\mathbb R,\\mathbb C\\}$, let $I,J$ be nonempty finite index sets with point assignments as in [[def-hlr-cluster-trees-and-geometric-admissibility]], and let $\\mathcal P$ be the block-cluster leaves of [[lem-hlr-block-cluster-leaves-partition-index-pairs]]. Fix an integer rank cap $k\\ge1$. An **H-matrix representation with cap $k$** assigns to each far block $b=(\\tau,\\sigma)\\in\\mathcal P$ factors $U_b\\in\\mathbb F^{\\tau\\times r_b}$ and $V_b\\in\\mathbb F^{\\sigma\\times r_b}$ with $0\\le r_b\\le k$, representing that block by $U_bV_b^*$. Each near block is stored as its full dense matrix $D_b\\in\\mathbb F^{\\tau\\times\\sigma}$. Since $\\mathcal P$ partitions $I\\times J$, these blocks determine a unique represented matrix $\\widetilde A\\in\\mathbb F^{I\\times J}$.\n\nThe rank cap on far blocks is an assumption about the representation or the target's approximability; geometric admissibility alone does not imply that a given matrix block has low rank. Near blocks remain dense and receive no low-rank guarantee.",
