  {
    "id": "thm-hlr-separated-cauchy-block-rank-bound",
    "kind": "theorem",
    "title": "A separated Cauchy block has a geometric rank bound",
    "statement": "Let $0\\le a<b$, $c\\ge0$, and choose $m,n\\ge1$ points $x_i\\in[0,a]$ and $y_j\\in[b,b+c]$. Put $q=a/b<1$ and define the Cauchy block $K_{ij}=1/(y_j-x_i)$. For any integer $r\\ge1$, the matrix $\\widetilde K$ with entries $\\widetilde K_{ij}=\\sum_{\\ell=0}^{r-1}x_i^\\ell y_j^{-(\\ell+1)}$ has rank at most $r$ and satisfies $|K_{ij}-\\widetilde K_{ij}|\\le q^r/(b-a)$ and $\\|K-\\widetilde K\\|_F\\le\\sqrt{mn}\\,q^r/(b-a)$. If $b-a\\ge\\eta\\max(a,c)$ and the H-matrix rank cap satisfies $k\\ge r$, the sampled clusters are admissible and $\\widetilde K$ is a permitted rank-at-most-$k$ far-block representation.",
