    "id": "thm-three-dimensional-laplace-expansion-error-and-work",
    "kind": "theorem",
    "title": "Three-dimensional Laplace cluster expansion error and work",
    "statement": "For the single-cluster evaluator of [[alg-three-dimensional-laplace-cluster-expansion]], let $Q=\\sum_{j\\in J}|q_j|$. If a target satisfies $r=|x-c|>a$, then its potential error obeys\n$$|U(x)-\\widetilde U_p(x)|\\le\\frac{Q}{r}\\frac{(a/r)^{p+1}}{1-a/r}.$$\nIf a target region is contained in a ball of radius $b$ centered at $d$, with $R=|d-c|>a+b$, then every target in the region has error at most\n$$\\frac{Q}{R-b}\\frac{\\bigl(a/(R-b)\\bigr)^{p+1}}{1-a/(R-b)}.$$\nLet $S=|J|$ and let $T_0$ be the number of targets. Under the monomial-coefficient evaluation convention of the algorithm, forming moments and evaluating the truncated field costs $O((S+T_0)(p+1)^4)$ arithmetic operations after the basis coefficient arrays have been prepared. If all basis values at sources and targets are supplied, the coefficient accumulation and expansion evaluation alone cost $O((S+T_0)(p+1)^2)$. This is a single-cluster estimate; it is not a 3D multilevel FMM tree complexity theorem.",
