      "description": "{\"scope\": [\"Define normalized finite MPS/TT state and Hermitian finite Hamiltonian supplied by physics; canonical QR gauge, one-site/two-site environment contractions, exact local eigenproblem, SVD split/truncate and finite sweeps/status.\", \"Prove mixed canonical gauge makes local norm Euclidean, environment defines Hermitian effective Hamiltonian, and exact local ground-state solve weakly decreases Rayleigh energy before truncation.\", \"Prove normalized-state energy difference <= 2||H|| ||psi-phi|| and use TT discarded weight to bound possible posttruncation increase. State stationarity conditional on bounded convergent exact sweeps; no universal global minimum, polynomial efficiency or spectral-gap promise.\"], \"proof_route\": \"Finite tensor contraction and isometry identities; Rayleigh variational inequality; Cauchy-Schwarz perturbation; distinguish bond adaptation from ideal fixed-bond block optimization.\", \"examples\": [\"Two-site spin-chain local solve\", \"Energy rises after aggressive truncation\", \"Degenerate local eigenspace with deterministic tie choice\"]}",
      "items": [
        {
          "id": "lem-na-finite-hermitian-ground-vector-with-deterministic-tie",
          "kind": "lemma",
          "title": "Finite Hermitian ground vectors and a deterministic tie rule",
          "statement": "Let $\\mathbb F\\in\\{\\mathbb R,\\mathbb C\\}$, let $m\\ge1$, and let $A\\in\\mathbb F^{m\\times m}$ satisfy $A^*=A$. Define\n$$q_A(x):=\\frac{x^*Ax}{x^*x}\\qquad(x\\in\\mathbb F^m\\setminus\\{0\\}).$$\nThen $q_A$ has a minimum $\\lambda_{\\min}$, equal to the least eigenvalue of $A$, and its minimizers are precisely the nonzero vectors in the lowest eigenspace $E_{\\min}=\\ker(A-\\lambda_{\\min}I)$. There is a deterministic choice of a unit minimizer: let $P_{\\min}$ be the orthogonal projection onto $E_{\\min}$, scan the standard basis $e_1,\\ldots,e_m$, and take the first $j$ with $P_{\\min}e_j\\ne0$. Normalize $P_{\\min}e_j$; over $\\mathbb C$, if $z$ is its first nonzero coordinate, multiply by $\\overline z/|z|$, and over $\\mathbb R$ multiply by $z/|z|$.",
          "deps": [
