        {
          "id": "ex-rational-transfer-function-pole-is-a-spurious-cleared-root",
          "kind": "example",
          "title": "A transfer-function pole becomes a spurious root after denominator clearing",
          "deps": [
            "def-rational-matrix-eigenproblem-and-pole-domain",
            "prop-clearing-a-common-denominator-away-from-poles"
          ],
          "justified_by": [],
          "dependency_level": 3,
          "statement": "The rational transfer matrix\n$$R(\\lambda)=\\begin{bmatrix}(\\lambda-1)^{-1}&0\\\\0&1\\end{bmatrix}$$\nhas a pole at $\\lambda=1$. Here a finite-dimensional state-space transfer matrix means a rational matrix of the form $C(\\lambda I-A)^{-1}B+D$; the displayed $R$ has such a realization with $A=[1]$, $B=[1\\ 0]$, $C=[1\\ 0]^T$, and $D=\\operatorname{diag}(0,1)$. It has no rational eigenpair on its domain, but clearing the common denominator yields a polynomial matrix singular at the excluded pole.",
