      "proof": "not-applicable"
    },
    "sources": {
      "references": [
        {
          "title": "NIST Digital Library of Mathematical Functions, §10.49, Explicit Formulas",
          "url": "https://dlmf.nist.gov/10.49",
          "locator": "§10.49(i), equations 10.49.1–10.49.6, terminating Laurent formulas for spherical Bessel and Hankel functions."
        },
        {
          "title": "NIST Digital Library of Mathematical Functions, §10.74, Methods of Computation",
          "url": "https://dlmf.nist.gov/10.74",
          "locator": "§10.74(i), exact spherical formulas and the cancellation risk when |z| is not large compared with the squared order."
        },
        {
          "title": "University of Toronto, Lecture Notes on Mathematical Methods 2019–20",
          "url": "https://www.physics.utoronto.ca/~phy1540f/p154019_cumullect.pdf",
          "locator": "§6.4, printed pp. 157–158, finite-derivative spherical formula and outgoing Hankel convention."
        }
      ]
    },
    "statement": "Inputs are those of [[def-spherical-bessel-evaluation-contract]], a nonempty finite list of valid targets, finite exact positive rational absolute tolerances, optional exact positive rational relative tolerances, and finite integer caps $W,Q,M_{\\max},p_{\\max}\\ge1$. $W$ is the maximum exact-arithmetic work count, $Q$ the maximum stage and represented-point interface index, $M_{\\max}$ the maximum Taylor degree, and $p_{\\max}$ the maximum final dyadic precision. A Gaussian rational is $a+ib$ with $a,b\\in\\mathbb Q$. A Laurent polynomial is a finite sum $\\sum_k a_k z^k$ with integer exponents and Gaussian-rational coefficients; its formal derivative uses $(z^k)'=kz^{k-1}$. An expression graph below is a finite directed acyclic graph of exact rational arithmetic operations.\n\nFactorials are those of [[def-factorial-and-falling-factorial]]; they are nonzero by that definition and [[lem-of-naturals-positive]], so every displayed factorial denominator is defined. For a rational $x$, $\\lceil x\\rceil$ denotes the least integer greater than or equal to $x$, computed by exact integer division.\n\nReject an invalid order, target, tolerance, cap, or interval input not contained in $(0,\\infty)$ with `domain`. For a represented point, an interval returned by the input interface may meet zero; such a stage is skipped and a later interface index is tried.\n\nConstruct Laurent polynomials by\n$$A_0=0,\\quad B_0=z^{-1},\\quad C_0=-iz^{-1},$$\n$$A_{r+1}=rz^{-1}A_r-A_r'-B_r,\\quad B_{r+1}=rz^{-1}B_r-B_r'+A_r,\\quad C_{r+1}=rz^{-1}C_r-C_r'-iC_r$$\nfor $r=0,\\ldots,\\ell-1$. With $s$ and $c$ denoting the sine and cosine input enclosures, form the unscaled outputs\n$$u=A_\\ell c+B_\\ell s,\\quad v=(A_\\ell'+B_\\ell)c+(B_\\ell'-A_\\ell)s,\\quad w=C_\\ell(c+is),\\quad t=(C_\\ell'+iC_\\ell)(c+is).$$\nForm the scaled outputs\n$$c_\\ell z^{-\\ell}u,\\quad c_\\ell z^{-\\ell}v,\\quad d_\\ell^{-1}z^{\\ell+1}C_\\ell,\\quad d_\\ell^{-1}z^{\\ell+1}(C_\\ell'+iC_\\ell).$$\nCollect like Laurent powers exactly; in particular cancel negative powers in the scaled $h_\\ell^{(1)}$ value when they cancel algebraically. A remaining $z^{-1}$ in the scaled derivative is evaluated only on positive input intervals.\n\nFor real intervals use exact rational endpoint hulls: $[a,b]+[c,d]=[a+c,b+d]$, $[a,b][c,d]=[\\min(ac,ad,bc,bd),\\max(ac,ad,bc,bd)]$, and $[a,b]^{-1}=[1/b,1/a]$ when $0<a\\le b$. For complex rectangles, apply these real interval operations to the real and imaginary components of $(x+iy)(u+iv)=(xu-yv)+i(xv+yu)$. Thus interval arithmetic encloses every pointwise sum and product; division is only by a positive power of the positive argument or by a positive integer constant. Compute powers, factorials, and rational endpoints by finite exact arithmetic.\n\nAt a positive current box $I=[a,b]$, choose $M\\ge0$ with $M+2\\ge2b$ and form the rational interval polynomial and rational tail\n$$T_M(I)=\\sum_{m=0}^{M}\\frac{(iI)^m}{m!},\\qquad E_M=\\frac{2b^{M+1}}{(M+1)!}.$$\nThe interval power is repeated complex-rectangle multiplication. Enclose $\\cos(I)$ by $\\operatorname{Re}T_M(I)+[-E_M,E_M]$ and $\\sin(I)$ by $\\operatorname{Im}T_M(I)+[-E_M,E_M]$. Evaluate each requested output graph with these leaves and $z\\in I$, and round each final endpoint outward to the grid $2^{-p}\\mathbb Z$.\n\nUse the following deterministic finite search. Charge the Laurent recurrence, graph construction, and coefficient collection to the work counter as they are performed. For stages $q=1,\\ldots,Q$, first take the fixed interval unchanged, or call the represented-point interface at index $q$. Skip a represented-point stage unless its returned interval has $a>0$. Set $p=q$ and $M=\\max(q,\\lceil2b\\rceil)$. If $p>p_{\\max}$ or $M>M_{\\max}$, stop with `inconclusive`. Evaluate all requested graphs and the displayed $T_M,E_M$ using the work counter: each exact rational or Gaussian-rational arithmetic operation or comparison, each Taylor term, each endpoint-rounding operation, and each interface call costs one unit. If the next operation would exceed $W$, stop with `inconclusive` before performing it. If the output format cannot represent a certified endpoint, return `range` with no finite claim. If every requested target has radius at most its absolute tolerance, return `success`; for each optional relative tolerance, set its flag true exactly when the enclosure excludes zero and the relative test in the contract passes. A failed relative test does not prevent absolute `success`. If no stage succeeds before a cap or the search ends, return `inconclusive`. No floating-point recurrence is used."
  },
