    },
    "sources": {
      "references": [
        {
          "title": "Trygve Helgaker, Molecular Integral Evaluation, 10th Sostrup Summer School lecture notes (2008)",
          "url": "https://trygvehelgaker.no/Presentations/Sostrup_Integrals_2008.pdf",
          "locator": "Complete 41-page PDF; primitive Cartesian Gaussians on pp. 3–6 and integral conventions on pp. 17–28."
        },
        {
          "title": "L. E. McMurchie and E. R. Davidson, One and Two Electron Integrals over Cartesian Gaussian Functions, UCRL-13791 (1976)",
          "url": "https://digital.library.unt.edu/ark:/67531/metadc1057442/m2/1/high_res_d/5184486.pdf",
          "locator": "Complete 23-page report; §§II–IV, especially pp. 5–18."
        }
      ]
    },
    "statement": "Write $\\mathbb N_0=\\{0,1,2,\\ldots\\}$ and $\\|x\\|_2=(x_1^2+x_2^2+x_3^2)^{1/2}$ for $x\\in\\mathbb R^3$. For $\\alpha=(\\alpha_1,\\alpha_2,\\alpha_3)\\in\\mathbb N_0^3$, exponent $a>0$, and center $A\\in\\mathbb R^3$, a **primitive Cartesian Gaussian** is\n$$g_{\\alpha,a,A}(x)=\\prod_{j=1}^3(x_j-A_j)^{\\alpha_j}\\exp(-a\\|x-A\\|_2^2),\\qquad x\\in\\mathbb R^3.$$\nA **finite Gaussian contraction** is a finite sum $\\phi=\\sum_{k=1}^q c_k g_{\\alpha_k,a_k,A_k}$ with $q\\in\\mathbb N_0$, $c_k\\in\\mathbb C$, $a_k>0$, and $A_k\\in\\mathbb R^3$. The zero-term sum is the zero function. Bras are conjugated.\n\nFor a pair of contractions put $f_{\\phi,\\psi}(x)=\\overline{\\phi(x)}\\psi(x)$. For pair densities $f,g$, define the **Coulomb form**\n$$\\mathcal C(f,g)=\\int_{\\mathbb R^3}\\int_{\\mathbb R^3}\\frac{\\overline{f(x)}g(y)}{\\|x-y\\|_2}\\,dy\\,dx.$$\nFor finite contractions $\\phi,\\psi,\\rho,\\sigma$, define the overlap, kinetic, point-nucleus attraction, and two-electron Coulomb expressions by\n$$S(\\phi,\\psi)=\\int_{\\mathbb R^3}\\overline{\\phi(x)}\\psi(x)\\,dx,\\qquad T(\\phi,\\psi)=-\\frac12\\int_{\\mathbb R^3}\\overline{\\phi(x)}\\,\\Delta\\psi(x)\\,dx,$$\n$$V_C(\\phi,\\psi)=\\int_{\\mathbb R^3}\\frac{\\overline{\\phi(x)}\\psi(x)}{\\|x-C\\|_2}\\,dx,$$\n$$G(\\phi,\\psi;\\rho,\\sigma)=\\mathcal C(f_{\\phi,\\psi},f_{\\rho,\\sigma}).$$\nThe primitives and their finite contractions are smooth: [[thm-derivative-of-exponential]] supplies smoothness of exp, and repeated coordinatewise applications of [[lem-derivative-algebra-and-chain-rules]] supply all polynomial-product and composition derivatives. Thus the following Laplacian is well defined.\n\nHere $\\Delta\\psi=\\sum_{j=1}^3\\partial_{x_j}^2\\psi$ is the Cartesian Laplacian.\nAll displayed integrals mean Lebesgue integrals in the sense of [[def-integrable-real-and-complex-functions-and-their-integrals]]. A finite nuclear attraction is any finite real linear combination of the $V_C$ terms, with its centers and weights specified. This definition alone asserts neither convergence nor a physical approximation postulate."
  },
