    "statement": "For pair densities $f,g$ arising as finite products of Gaussian contractions in [[def-cartesian-gaussian-primitives-and-contractions]], let\n$$\\mathcal C(f,g)=\\int_{\\mathbb R^3}\\int_{\\mathbb R^3}\\frac{\\overline{f(x)}g(y)}{\\|x-y\\|_2}\\,dy\\,dx.$$\nThen $\\mathcal C$ is a well-defined Hermitian positive-semidefinite sesquilinear form on their finite linear span, and\n$$|\\mathcal C(f,g)|^2\\le\\mathcal C(f,f)\\mathcal C(g,g).$$\nFor orbitals $\\phi_a,\\phi_b,\\phi_c,\\phi_d$ and pair densities $f_{ab}=\\overline{\\phi_a}\\phi_b$, the integral convention in [[def-cartesian-gaussian-primitives-and-contractions]] gives\n$$|G(\\phi_a,\\phi_b;\\phi_c,\\phi_d)|\\le\\sqrt{G(\\phi_a,\\phi_b;\\phi_a,\\phi_b)G(\\phi_c,\\phi_d;\\phi_c,\\phi_d)},$$\nwhere each diagonal quantity is nonnegative. For the real finite energy functional in [[def-finite-gaussian-basis-variational-energy-and-gradient]], let $C\\in\\mathbb R^{n\\times r}$, $D\\in\\mathbb R^{r\\times r}$, and define the input-basis density coefficients $W_{ab}=\\sum_{p,q=1}^r C_{ap}D_{pq}C_{bq}$. Here the $n$ input basis functions may themselves be contractions; a primitive basis is the singleton-contraction special case. Reindexing the finite two-body energy gives $E_2=\\tfrac12\\sum_{a,b,c,d}W_{ab}W_{cd}G_{ab,cd}$. Thus for any finite set $S$ of input-basis quartets omitted from that contraction,\n$$B_S=\\frac12\\sum_{(a,b,c,d)\\in S}|W_{ab}W_{cd}|\\sqrt{G_{ab,ab}G_{cd,cd}}$$\nis an accumulated upper bound for the absolute omitted energy contribution. This does not assert that a screened integral is zero."
