      ],
      "description": "{\"scope\": \"For f in C3 on an open admissible set G in Rm, define strictly hyperbolic conservation systems and admissible-state set, characteristic families, Rankine–Hugoniot and Lax admissibility as definitions. Prove the local small-data Riemann solver near a state where each field is genuinely nonlinear or linearly degenerate, with clearly bounded neighborhood; do not promise arbitrary large-data solutions. Define Godunov, Roe and HLL fluxes. Prove consistency and conservation; Roe property is an algebraic identity, not entropy admissibility. For ideal-gas Euler define pressure mathematically on rho>0, E>|m|²/(2rho), derive eigenstructure and give admissibility/failure conditions; HLL wave-speed bounds are explicit hypotheses or separately proved bounds.\", \"proof_route\": \"Prove eigenpair smoothness under simple spectrum, local rarefaction integral curves, shock curves using an augmented Rankine–Hugoniot implicit-function argument, tangent matching and wave-composition invertibility; verify weak jumps. Prove HLL flux by integrating the two-wave approximate conservation balance. Derive Euler sound speed and contact eigenvector locally. An exact Riemann procedure uses a bracketed pressure root with finite iteration budget and vacuum detection; existence/uniqueness of that root is a separate lemma.\", \"examples\": \"acoustic system, Sod tube, contact, Roe expansion shock/vacuum failure.\"}",
      "items": [
  {
    "id": "def-hyperbolic-system-and-characteristic-families",
    "kind": "definition",
    "title": "Strictly hyperbolic conservation systems and characteristic families",
    "deps": [
      "def-c-one-map-and-local-inverse",
      "def-ck-euclidean-maps-and-diffeomorphisms"
    ],
    "dependency_level": 0,
    "statement": "Let $m\\ge1$, let $G\\subseteq\\mathbb R^m$ be open, and let\n$f:G\\to\\mathbb R^m$ be $C^3$ ([[def-c-one-map-and-local-inverse]];\nthe $C^3$ convention is that of [[def-ck-euclidean-maps-and-diffeomorphisms]]).\nThe one-dimensional conservation system with state domain $G$ is\n$$u_t+\\partial_x f(u)=0,\\qquad u(t,x)\\in G,$$\nwhere $u$ is the conserved state and $f$ its flux. Its flux Jacobian is\n$A(u)=Df(u)$. The system is **strictly hyperbolic on an open set $N\\subseteq G$**\nif, for every $u\\in N$, $A(u)$ has $m$ distinct real eigenvalues, written in\nincreasing order $\\lambda_1(u)<\\cdots<\\lambda_m(u)$. An **$i$th characteristic\nfamily** on $N$ is a $C^2$ right-eigenvector field $r_i:N\\to\\mathbb R^m\\setminus\\{0\\}$\nwith $A(u)r_i(u)=\\lambda_i(u)r_i(u)$. It is **genuinely nonlinear** on $N$ if\n$D\\lambda_i(u)r_i(u)$ is nowhere zero there; after shrinking $N$ its sign is\nconstant. It is **linearly degenerate** on $N$ if $D\\lambda_i(u)r_i(u)=0$\nthroughout $N$. Rescaling $r_i$ by a nonzero constant changes the wave-strength\nparameter but not the family.\n\nFor $Q=(0,\\infty)\\times\\mathbb R$, a locally bounded map $u:Q\\to G$ is a\n**distributional weak solution with initial data $u_0$** if\n$$\\int_Q\\bigl(u\\cdot\\varphi_t+f(u)\\cdot\\varphi_x\\bigr)\\,dt\\,dx=0$$\nfor every vector test function $\\varphi\\in C_c^\\infty(Q;\\mathbb R^m)$ and\n$u(t,\\cdot)\\to u_0$ in $L^1_{\\rm loc}(\\mathbb R;\\mathbb R^m)$ as\n$t\\downarrow0$. A **Riemann initial datum** is the step\n$u_0(x)=u_L$ for $x<0$ and $u_0(x)=u_R$ for $x>0$.\n\nIn this weak-solution clause, $u$ is required to be Lebesgue measurable and\n$f\\circ u\\in L^1_{\\rm loc}(Q;\\mathbb R^m)$, so the test-function integral is\ndefined. Local boundedness gives $u\\in L^1_{\\rm loc}(Q;\\mathbb R^m)$.",
    "justified_by": [],
    "provenance": {
      "statement": "literature-derived",
      "proof": "not-applicable"
    },
    "sources": {
      "references": [
        {
          "title": "Alberto Bressan, Hyperbolic Conservation Laws: An Illustrated Tutorial",
          "url": "https://yulia-petrova.github.io/teaching/shocks/Bressan-tutorial.pdf",
          "locator": "§§3.1–3.3, printed pp. 21–26: strict hyperbolicity and characteristic families"
        }
      ]
    }
  },
  {
    "id": "def-rankine-hugoniot-and-lax-shocks",
    "kind": "definition",
    "title": "Rankine–Hugoniot jumps and Lax shocks",
    "deps": [
      "def-hyperbolic-system-and-characteristic-families"
    ],
    "dependency_level": 1,
    "statement": "For a system as in [[def-hyperbolic-system-and-characteristic-families]], a\ndiscontinuity with constant states $u_L,u_R\\in G$ and speed $s\\in\\mathbb R$ is\na **Rankine–Hugoniot jump** if\n$$f(u_R)-f(u_L)=s(u_R-u_L).$$\nFor a system strictly hyperbolic on $N\\subseteq G$, states $u_L,u_R\\in N$,\nand $1\\le i\\le m$, it is a **strict Lax $i$-shock** if it is a Rankine–Hugoniot jump and\n$$\\lambda_j(u_L)<s\\ (j<i),\\qquad \\lambda_i(u_R)<s<\\lambda_i(u_L),\\qquad s<\\lambda_j(u_R)\\ (j>i).$$\nAn empty family of inequalities is omitted. A linearly-degenerate contact has\nequal $i$-speeds on both sides, so it is not a strict Lax shock.",
    "justified_by": [],
    "provenance": {
      "statement": "literature-derived",
      "proof": "not-applicable"
    },
    "sources": {
      "references": [
        {
          "title": "Alberto Bressan, Hyperbolic Conservation Laws: An Illustrated Tutorial",
          "url": "https://yulia-petrova.github.io/teaching/shocks/Bressan-tutorial.pdf",
          "locator": "§§2.2–2.3, printed pp. 13–15: Rankine–Hugoniot and Lax admissibility"
        },
        {
          "title": "Randall J. LeVeque, Numerical Methods for Conservation Laws",
          "url": "https://tevza.org/home/course/modelling-II_2016/books/Leveque%20-%20Numerical%20Methods%20for%20Conservation%20Laws.pdf",
          "locator": "Ch. 7 §§7.1, 7.4, printed pp. 70–78: Hugoniot locus and Lax entropy condition"
        }
      ]
    }
  },
  {
    "id": "lem-smooth-simple-characteristic-eigenpairs",
    "kind": "lemma",
    "title": "Smooth local eigenpairs for a simple characteristic spectrum",
    "deps": [
      "def-hyperbolic-system-and-characteristic-families",
      "thm-euclidean-implicit-function-theorem"
    ],
    "dependency_level": 1,
    "statement": "Let $f:G\\to\\mathbb R^m$ be $C^3$ and let $\\bar u\\in G$ be a state at which\n$A(\\bar u)=Df(\\bar u)$ has distinct real eigenvalues\n$\\lambda_1^0<\\cdots<\\lambda_m^0$. For each $i$, there is a neighborhood $N_i$\nof $\\bar u$ and $C^2$ functions $\\lambda_i:N_i\\to\\mathbb R$ and\n$r_i:N_i\\to\\mathbb R^m$ such that $A(u)r_i(u)=\\lambda_i(u)r_i(u)$,\n$\\lambda_i(\\bar u)=\\lambda_i^0$, and the eigenvectors can be normalized by a\nfixed linear functional. After intersecting these finitely many neighborhoods,\nthe eigenvalues remain real, simple, and ordered, and $r_1(u),\\ldots,r_m(u)$\nform a basis at each $u$.",
