  {
    "id": "def-ideal-gas-euler-state-and-pressure",
    "kind": "definition",
    "title": "Admissible states and pressure for one-dimensional ideal-gas Euler",
    "deps": [
      "def-hyperbolic-system-and-characteristic-families"
    ],
    "dependency_level": 1,
    "statement": "Fix $\\gamma>1$. In one space dimension the conserved state is\n$U=(\\rho,m,E)\\in\\mathbb R^3$, where $\\rho$ is density, $m$ momentum, and\n$E$ total energy per unit volume. The **admissible-state set** is the open set\n$$\\mathcal G_\\gamma=\\{(\\rho,m,E):\\rho>0,\\ E>m^2/(2\\rho)\\}.$$\nThe ideal-gas pressure on this set is\n$$p(U)=(\\gamma-1)\\left(E-\\frac{m^2}{2\\rho}\\right)>0,$$\nthe velocity is $u=m/\\rho$, and the flux is\n$$F(U)=\\left(m,\\frac{m^2}{\\rho}+p,\\frac{m}{\\rho}(E+p)\\right).$$\nThe sound speed is $c=\\sqrt{\\gamma p/\\rho}$. These definitions apply only\non $\\mathcal G_\\gamma$; a state with nonpositive density or nonpositive\ninternal energy is inadmissible. The set is open because $\\rho$ and\n$E-m^2/(2\\rho)$ are continuous on $\\rho>0$ and the inequalities are strict;\nall displayed flux functions are smooth on this domain.",
    "justified_by": [],
    "provenance": {
      "statement": "literature-derived",
      "proof": "not-applicable"
    },
    "sources": {
      "references": [
        {
          "title": "Randall J. LeVeque, Numerical Methods for Conservation Laws",
          "url": "https://tevza.org/home/course/modelling-II_2016/books/Leveque%20-%20Numerical%20Methods%20for%20Conservation%20Laws.pdf",
          "locator": "Chs. 5 and 9, printed pp. 48–53 and 89–93: polytropic Euler variables and Riemann problem"
        }
      ]
    }
  },
  {
    "id": "def-roe-linearization-and-flux",
    "kind": "definition",
    "title": "Roe linearization and Roe numerical flux",
    "deps": [
      "def-hyperbolic-system-and-characteristic-families"
    ],
    "dependency_level": 1,
    "statement": "For states $u_L,u_R$ in the open state domain $G$ of\n[[def-hyperbolic-system-and-characteristic-families]], a **Roe matrix** is a map\n$\\widetilde A:G\\times G\\to\\mathbb R^{m\\times m}$ such that, for all\n$u,u_L,u_R\\in G$,\n$$\\widetilde A(u,u)=Df(u),\\qquad \\widetilde A(u_L,u_R)(u_R-u_L)=f(u_R)-f(u_L),$$\nand $\\widetilde A(u_L,u_R)$ is diagonalizable over $\\mathbb R$ with real\neigenvalues. The map $\\widetilde A$ is also continuous at each diagonal pair\n$(u,u)$. If $\\widetilde A=R\\Lambda R^{-1}$, define\n$|\\widetilde A|=R|\\Lambda|R^{-1}$ by replacing each diagonal eigenvalue with\nits absolute value. The **Roe flux associated with this matrix** is\n$$\\widehat f_{\\rm Roe}(u_L,u_R)=\\tfrac12(f(u_L)+f(u_R))-\\tfrac12|\\widetilde A(u_L,u_R)|(u_R-u_L).$$\nThe jump identity is an algebraic conservation property. This definition does\nnot assert entropy admissibility of the approximate wave pattern.",
    "justified_by": [],
    "provenance": {
      "statement": "literature-derived",
      "proof": "not-applicable"
    },
    "sources": {
      "references": [
        {
          "title": "Randall J. LeVeque, Numerical Methods for Conservation Laws",
          "url": "https://tevza.org/home/course/modelling-II_2016/books/Leveque%20-%20Numerical%20Methods%20for%20Conservation%20Laws.pdf",
          "locator": "Ch. 14 §14.2, printed pp. 148–151: Roe matrix, jump property, and numerical flux"
        }
      ]
    }
  },
  {
    "id": "def-hll-two-wave-flux",
    "kind": "definition",
    "title": "HLL two-wave approximate flux",
    "deps": [
      "def-hyperbolic-system-and-characteristic-families"
    ],
    "dependency_level": 1,
    "statement": "For a two-state problem for a flux $f$ on the open state domain $G$ of\n[[def-hyperbolic-system-and-characteristic-families]], choose explicit speeds\n$S_L<S_R$. A signal speed means the propagation speed of one wave in the\nparticular Riemann solution being approximated. If the HLL model is used as an\nenclosing approximation to that fan, its admissibility hypothesis is that\nevery such speed lies in $[S_L,S_R]$; this definition supplies no general\nwave-speed bound.\nSet $f_L=f(u_L)$ and $f_R=f(u_R)$. The HLL flux is\n$$\\widehat f_{\\rm HLL}=\\begin{cases}f_L,&0\\le S_L,\\\\\\dfrac{S_Rf_L-S_Lf_R+S_LS_R(u_R-u_L)}{S_R-S_L},&S_L<0<S_R,\\\\f_R,&S_R\\le0.\\end{cases}$$\nIn the middle case the model has three constant regions separated by the two\nwaves $S_L,S_R$ and one intermediate state. The wave-speed enclosure is a\nseparate hypothesis whenever an approximation claim uses it.",
    "justified_by": [],
    "provenance": {
      "statement": "literature-derived",
      "proof": "not-applicable"
    },
    "sources": {
      "references": [
