        {
          "id": "thm-na-linear-monotone-first-order-barrier",
          "kind": "theorem",
          "title": "A monotone linear one-step advection scheme cannot be second order at noninteger CFL",
          "deps": [],
          "dependency_level": 0,
          "statement": "Let $a\\ne0$, $h>0$ and $\\Delta t>0$, and put $\\nu=a\\Delta t/h$. Fix a noninteger $\\nu$. Consider a finite-stencil, translation-invariant linear one-step scheme\n$$U_j^{n+1}=\\sum_{\\ell=-r}^{r}c_\\ell U_{j+\\ell}^n$$\nwith real coefficients that may depend on the fixed $\\nu$ but are independent of $h$, $j$ and $n$. Assume the scheme is conservative, is consistent with $u_t+a u_x=0$, and is second-order accurate in the local truncation sense. For these fixed coefficients, second-order consistency requires exact matching of the constant, linear and quadratic Taylor moments for the one-step translation. If the update is monotone in the componentwise order on grid sequences, then these assumptions are inconsistent. Thus at a noninteger Courant number no such linear monotone scheme can be second order.",
