        {
          "id": "def-pe03-system-entropy-pair-and-potential",
          "kind": "definition",
          "title": "Convex entropy pairs, entropy variables, and entropy potentials for systems",
          "deps": [
            "def-hyperbolic-system-and-characteristic-families"
          ],
          "dependency_level": 1,
          "statement": "Use the conservation-system convention of\n[[def-hyperbolic-system-and-characteristic-families]]: on an open convex state\ndomain $\\mathcal A\\subseteq\\mathbb R^m$, the flux is $F:\\mathcal A\\to\\mathbb R^m$.\nA **convex entropy pair** is a pair $(\\eta,q)$ with $\\eta\\in C^2(\\mathcal A)$,\n$q\\in C^1(\\mathcal A)$, positive-semidefinite Hessian $D^2\\eta$, and\n$$Dq(U)=D\\eta(U)\\,DF(U)\\qquad(U\\in\\mathcal A).$$\nThe **entropy variable** is the column vector $v(U)=\\nabla\\eta(U)$; gradients\nare represented as columns, so the compatibility equation above means\n$\\nabla q(U)=DF(U)^T v(U)$. The **entropy potential** is\n$\\psi(U)=v(U)^T F(U)-q(U)$. This definition does not assert that every convex\n$\\eta$ admits a flux $q$; the compatibility equation is part of the hypothesis.",
          "strategy": "State derivative and column-vector conventions; no existence of q is asserted.",
          "justified_by": [],
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "literature-derived",
            "proof": "not-applicable"
