        {
          "id": "alg-pe03-convex-scaling-positivity-limiter",
          "kind": "algorithm",
          "title": "Maximal convex scaling toward an admissible cell mean",
          "deps": [
            "def-pe03-convex-invariant-domain"
          ],
          "dependency_level": 1,
          "statement": "Let $K\\subseteq\\mathbb R^m$ be closed and convex (convex in the sense of\n[[def-pe03-convex-invariant-domain]]), let $J$ be a finite nonempty\nindex set, let $w_j>0$ with $\\sum_{j\\in J}w_j=1$, and let input states\n$V_j\\in\\mathbb R^m$ have mean $\\bar V=\\sum_jw_jV_j\\in K$. The **maximal\nconvex scaling limiter** returns the largest $\\theta\\in[0,1]$ for which every\n$$V_j^\\theta=\\bar V+\\theta(V_j-\\bar V)$$\nbelongs to $K$, together with all the states $V_j^\\theta$. Equivalently, form\n$$S=\\{\\theta\\in[0,1]:V_j^\\theta\\in K\\text{ for every }j\\in J\\},$$\nand return $\\theta_*:=\\max S$ and $(V_j^{\\theta_*})_{j\\in J}$. The mean is\nassumed admissible; implementation, cost, and any pointwise-in-cell property\nare not part of this definition.",
          "strategy": "Specify the finite input, output, feasible set, and maximum selection; make no complexity claim.",
          "justified_by": [],
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "literature-derived",
            "proof": "not-applicable"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
