      ],
      "description": "{\"scope\": \"Define H(div), weak normal trace by Green duality, RT0/BDM spaces, contravariant Piola map, divergence-commuting interpolation on its proper regularity domain, and a stable RT0/P0 mixed Darcy method. Prove mixed well-posedness and flux/pressure error, exact element mass balance and normal-flux continuity. Define hybridization/static condensation and prove equivalence modulo pressure gauge. Coefficient-robust claims explicitly track permeability contrast and weighted norms.\", \"proof_route\": \"Prove Lipschitz-cell normal trace boundedness using an H1 lifting and Green independence; construct RT unisolvence and divergence commuting from face moments. Construct a bounded commuting projection using smoothing when canonical moments are not defined on all H(div); prove discrete inf-sup from divergence lifting plus interpolation. Apply NA34 abstract mixed theorem; hybridization follows local elimination and face multiplier equations.\", \"examples\": \"constant-flux triangle, checkerboard permeability, mixed versus primal nonconservative postprocessing, hybrid local elimination.\"}",
      "items": [
  {
    "id": "def-na-contravariant-piola-map",
    "kind": "definition",
    "title": "Contravariant Piola map",
    "statement": "Let $\\widehat K,K\\subset\\mathbb R^d$ be nondegenerate open simplices and let $F_K:\\widehat K\\to K$ be the orientation-preserving affine bijection $F_K(\\widehat x)=A_K\\widehat x+b_K$, where $J_K:=\\det A_K>0$. For a measurable vector field $\\widehat v$ on $\\widehat K$, define its contravariant Piola push-forward by\n$$\n(\\mathcal P_K\\widehat v)(x):=J_K^{-1}A_K\\widehat v(F_K^{-1}(x)),\\qquad x\\in K.\n$$\nThe inverse pull-back is $\\widehat v(\\widehat x)=J_KA_K^{-1}v(F_K(\\widehat x))$. Facet normals are oriented consistently with the chosen orientation of $F_K$; the associated oriented surface vector transforms by $n\\,ds=J_KA_K^{-T}\\widehat n\\,d\\widehat s$.",
    "deps": [],
    "dependency_level": 0,
    "strategy": "State the map, domains, orientation, and inverse convention.",
    "justified_by": [],
    "provenance": {
