          "statement": "Let $\\mathcal T$ be a finite matching affine tetrahedral mesh. The four face-normal fluxes are unisolvent on each $RT_0(K)$, and matching face fluxes assemble a global $H(\\operatorname{div})$ field. For smooth scalar $f$, smooth vector fields $u,v$, and the canonical interpolants determined by vertex values, oriented edge circulations, oriented face-normal fluxes, and cell integrals, the interpolation maps satisfy cellwise\n$$\\nabla I_h^0f=I_h^1\\nabla f,\\qquad \\operatorname{curl}I_h^1u=I_h^2\\operatorname{curl}u,\\qquad \\operatorname{div}I_h^2v=I_h^3\\operatorname{div}v.$$\nAny assignment of one real oriented value to each global mesh edge determines a unique assembled field in $N_h^1$; any assignment of one real oriented flux to each global mesh face determines a unique assembled field in $N_h^2$. These assemblies belong to $H(\\operatorname{curl};\\Omega)$ and $H(\\operatorname{div};\\Omega)$, respectively, with the global spaces and conventions of [[def-na-lowest-order-whitney-de-rham-spaces]]. Applying the local moment identities to polynomial cell restrictions also proves that the global finite-element spaces form the subcomplex $W_h^0\\xrightarrow{\\nabla}N_h^1\\xrightarrow{\\operatorname{curl}}N_h^2\\xrightarrow{\\operatorname{div}}N_h^3$. The canonical interpolation identities themselves apply to smooth inputs; they do not extend the canonical edge moments to arbitrary $H(\\operatorname{curl})$ inputs."
