    },
    "statement": "Let $F=\\{(x_0,y,z):y_0\\le y\\le y_1,\\ z_0\\le z\\le z_1\\}$ be an oriented rectangle with normal $e_x$, and let $E$ be a $C^1$ vector field near $F$. Orient $\\partial F$ by the outward-normal-first convention. Then\n$$\\int_{\\partial F}E\\cdot t\\,ds=\\int_F(\\operatorname{curl}E)\\cdot e_x\\,dS=\\int_{y_0}^{y_1}\\int_{z_0}^{z_1}(\\partial_yE_z-\\partial_zE_y)(x_0,y,z)\\,dz\\,dy.$$\nThe identities for normals $e_y$ and $e_z$ follow by cyclic permutation of coordinates. Reversing a face orientation reverses both integrals."
  },
  {
    "id": "def-transient-maxwell-system-with-material-interfaces",
    "kind": "definition",
    "title": "Transient Maxwell system with piecewise materials and boundary conditions",
    "deps": [
      "def-na-zero-tangential-hcurl"
    ],
    "dependency_level": 2,
    "strategy": "State the real-field first-order Maxwell system, weighted energy spaces, charge continuity, PEC and periodic boundaries, and distributional interface convention without differentiating discontinuous coefficients.",
    "justified_by": [],
    "provenance": {
      "statement": "literature-derived",
      "proof": "not-applicable"
    },
    "sources": {
      "references": [
        {
          "title": "John B. Schneider, Understanding the Finite-Difference Time-Domain Method",
          "url": "https://eecs.wsu.edu/~schneidj/ufdtd/ufdtd.pdf",
          "locator": "Chapter 2 §§2.8–2.14, printed pp. 21–26: electric and magnetic boundary conditions, conductivity, PEC, and the time-varying Maxwell equations."
        }
      ]
    },
    "statement": "Let $\\Omega\\subset\\mathbb R^3$ be a bounded Lipschitz polyhedron, or a periodic rectangular cell. The real electric field is $E(t,x)\\in\\mathbb R^3$ and the magnetic flux density is $B(t,x)\\in\\mathbb R^3$; the magnetic intensity is $H=\\mu^{-1}B$. Let $\\epsilon(x)$ and $\\mu(x)$ be measurable, time-independent, symmetric matrix fields for which there are constants $0<\\epsilon_0\\le\\epsilon_1$ and $0<\\mu_0\\le\\mu_1$ with\n$$\\epsilon_0|v|^2\\le v^T\\epsilon(x)v\\le\\epsilon_1|v|^2,\\qquad \\mu_0|v|^2\\le v^T\\mu(x)v\\le\\mu_1|v|^2$$\nfor almost every $x$ and every $v\\in\\mathbb R^3$. A conductivity tensor $\\sigma(x)$, when present, is measurable, symmetric, and positive semidefinite. The coefficients may jump across finitely many Lipschitz material interfaces. No derivative of a coefficient is part of the model.\n\nIn each material region the constitutive fields satisfy\n$$\\partial_t(\\epsilon E)=\\operatorname{curl}(\\mu^{-1}B)-J_{\\rm src}-\\sigma E,\\qquad \\partial_t B+\\operatorname{curl}E=0.$$\nHere $J_{\\rm src}$ is the impressed free-current density, while $\\sigma E$ is the conductive current. Electric charge $\\rho$ is constrained by $\\operatorname{div}(\\epsilon E)=\\rho$ and the total-current continuity equation $\\partial_t\\rho+\\operatorname{div}(J_{\\rm src}+\\sigma E)=0$; the magnetic constraint is $\\operatorname{div}B=0$. These equalities are distributional. In the lossless branch set $\\sigma=0$.\n\nThe boundary choice is one of the following. A **periodic boundary** identifies opposite traces of the rectangular cell. A **perfect electric conductor (PEC) boundary** imposes zero tangential trace $n\\times E=0$ on $\\partial\\Omega$. At a material interface with no singular surface charge or current, the weak equations are understood with the transmission conditions $[n\\times E]=0$, $[n\\cdot B]=0$, $[n\\times(\\mu^{-1}B)]=0$, and $[n\\cdot(\\epsilon E)]=0$, where $[q]$ is the trace from one side minus the trace from the other. The interface conditions are part of the model convention; no smooth Taylor expansion across a coefficient jump is asserted.\n\nFor PEC, the weak Ampere equation is $(\\epsilon E_t,v)_{L^2}-(\\mu^{-1}B,\\operatorname{curl}v)_{L^2}=-(J_{\\rm src}+\\sigma E,v)_{L^2}$ for every $v\\in H_0(\\operatorname{curl};\\Omega)$ as defined in [[def-na-zero-tangential-hcurl]]; the periodic branch uses periodic $H(\\operatorname{curl})$ test fields. Faraday’s equation remains $B_t+\\operatorname{curl}E=0$ in distributions. These weak forms define the test-space and boundary convention used by the edge finite element method.\n\nThe weighted field norms are $\\|E\\|_\\epsilon^2=\\int_\\Omega E^T\\epsilon E\\,dx$ and $\\|B\\|_{\\mu^{-1}}^2=\\int_\\Omega B^T\\mu^{-1}B\\,dx$. The uniform coefficient bounds make them equivalent to the ordinary $L^2$ norms. All later smooth-error claims require smoothness separately on each mesh-aligned material region."
  },
  {
    "id": "def-maxwell-discrete-cochain-and-yee-grid",
    "kind": "definition",
    "title": "Oriented Maxwell cochains, masses, and the Yee grid",
    "deps": [
