  {
    "id": "thm-tpfa-green-coercivity-maximum-principle",
    "kind": "theorem",
    "title": "TPFA discrete Green identity, coercivity, and maximum principle",
    "statement": "Let $\\mathcal T$ be a finite TPFA-admissible cell-face mesh, let every cell be connected through interior faces to a cell with a boundary face, and let $T_F>0$ be the transmissibilities of [[def-tpfa-harmonic-face-scheme]]. For cell vectors $u,v$, define\n$$ (A_hu)_K=\\frac1{|K|}\\left(\\sum_{F=K|L}T_F(u_K-u_L)+\\sum_{F\\subset\\partial K\\cap\\partial\\Omega}T_{K,F}u_K\\right). $$\nThen interior fluxes are locally conservative, and for all cell vectors $u,v$,\n$$ \\sum_{K\\in\\mathcal K}|K|v_K(A_hu)_K= \\sum_{F=K|L\\in\\mathcal F_{\\rm int}}T_F(v_K-v_L)(u_K-u_L) +\\sum_{F\\in\\mathcal F_{\\rm bdy}}T_{K,F}v_Ku_K. $$\nThe stiffness matrix associated with the displayed bilinear identity is symmetric positive definite. Equivalently, $A_h$ is self-adjoint and positive definite in the volume-weighted inner product $\\langle u,v\\rangle_h=\\sum_K|K|u_Kv_K$; its ordinary coordinate matrix need not be symmetric when cell volumes differ. Hence every finite source and boundary-data vector gives a unique solution. If all cell sources and all prescribed boundary face values are nonnegative, then every solution cell value is nonnegative. The maximum-principle claim uses the boundary anchor; no diagonal sign statement alone is used as a substitute for that condition.",
