      "category": "numerical-pdes-and-spatial-discretization",
      "label": "PE25",
      "title": "Overlapping Schwarz and stable subspace decompositions",
      "companion": "overlapping-schwarz-and-stable-subspace-decompositions-examples",
      "requires": [
        "multigrid-methods-and-complexity",
        "ceas-lemma-and-aubin-nitsche-error-estimates",
        "mixed-finite-elements-and-stokes-stability"
      ],
      "description": "{\"scope\": \"Define local restriction/prolongation, additive and multiplicative Schwarz, exact/inexact local solves and a coarse space for SPD conforming diffusion. Prove the abstract additive Schwarz condition bound from a stable decomposition constant C0 and a strengthened Cauchy–Schwarz/overlap constant C1; give the precise norm and normalization so the bound is C0*C1. Prove multiplicative contraction from the associated subspace correction estimate with its actual constants. Then prove a two-level overlapping P1 Poisson specialization on quasiuniform shape-regular meshes, shape-regular subdomains, coarse diameter H and overlap delta, with explicit H/delta dependence and bounded overlap. A one-level method does not receive a uniform-in-subdomain-count bound. High coefficient contrast and anisotropy require separately constructed robust coarse spaces; the ordinary partition proof does not imply robustness.\", \"proof_route\": \"Local energy minimization expresses B inverse as the minimum summed local energy over decompositions. Lower/upper Rayleigh bounds follow from stable decomposition and bounded synthesis, using existing SPD/CG suppliers. Construct a boundary-preserving coarse quasi-interpolant (NA28 lower-regularity interface) and partition-of-unity theta_i with ||grad theta_i||≤C/delta; decompose u into coarse part plus interpolated theta_i(u-u0), then prove local interpolation/overlap bounds and stable energy estimates. Multiplicative correction uses exact projection error products, telescoped energy decreases and a proved stable-decomposition estimate; noncommuting projections cannot simply multiply scalar contraction factors. Inexact local solvers require uniform spectral equivalence and resulting modified constants.\", \"examples\": \"one- versus two-level 1D Poisson, overlap shrinking, coefficient-channel failure of a naive coarse space, exact/inexact local solves.\"}",
      "items": [
        {
          "id": "lem-schwarz-p1-product-cutoff-interpolation-energy-estimate",
