        {
          "id": "lem-fas-second-derivative-of-composed-cycle-map",
          "kind": "lemma",
          "title": "Second derivative formula for a composed cycle map",
          "statement": "Let $X_0,X_1,X_2,X_3$ be finite-dimensional real Euclidean spaces and let\n$U_i\\subseteq X_i$ be open for $i=0,1,2$. Suppose $S:U_0\\to U_1$, $C:U_1\\to U_2$, and\n$T:U_2\\to X_3$ are $C^2$, meaning that each is totally differentiable with\ncontinuous derivative and that its derivative map is itself totally\ndifferentiable. Write $\\mathcal L(X,Y)$ for the finite-dimensional space of\nlinear maps $X\\to Y$ with the induced Euclidean operator norm. Put\n$E=T\\circ C\\circ S$. For a $C^2$ map $f$, define\n$D^2f(x)[h,k]:=(D(Df)(x)h)k$. At $x\\in U_0$, set $y=S(x)$ and\n$z=C(y)$. Then for all $h,k\\in X_0$,\n$$D^2E(x)[h,k]=D^2T(z)[DC(y)DS(x)h,DC(y)DS(x)k]+DT(z)\\bigl(D^2C(y)[DS(x)h,DS(x)k]+DC(y)D^2S(x)[h,k]\\bigr).$$",
          "deps": [
            "cor-finite-dimensional-normed-spaces-are-banach",
            "thm-chain-sum-product-and-composition-rules-for-banach-derivatives"
          ],
          "dependency_level": 0,
          "local_order": 2,
          "strategy": "Differentiate the first-derivative composition identity using the bounded bilinear product rule, then apply the result twice.",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "literature-derived",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "Chin-Lung Wang, Banach Calculus, §§2.1.1–2.1.4 and §2.3",
                "url": "https://www.math.ntu.edu.tw/~dragon/Lecture%20Notes/Banach%20Calculus%202012.pdf"
              }
            ]
          },
          "justified_by": []
