      ],
      "description": "{\"scope\": \"**Exact scope.** Symmetric normalized compactly supported kernel W_ε and cell/particle quadrature with fill distance and bounded cell volumes. Prove convolution bias O(ε²) for C² functions from first-moment cancellation, and derivative bias under corresponding higher regularity; bound particle quadrature error explicitly by a cellwise oscillation sum or Lipschitz norm. State the joint refinement relation between particle fill distance and ε required for convergence; fixed neighbors/irregular particles do not automatically give consistent derivatives. Boundary truncation destroys unity/linear reproduction: give a specified corrected moment fit with uniformly invertible moment matrix, or restrict theorem to interior/periodic points and identify the boundary solver as a separate obligation. Do not claim arbitrary boundary consistency.\\n\\nThe specified SPH system has `ρ_i=Σ_j m_j W_ε(x_i−x_j)`, `x_i′=v_i`, and `v_i′=−Σ_j m_j(p_i/ρ_i²+p_j/ρ_j²)∇W_ε(x_i−x_j)`, positive masses, a C² radial kernel and a proved lower bound on ρ_i on the controlled tube. If internal energies are included, use exactly `e_i′=(p_i/ρ_i²)Σ_j m_j(v_i−v_j)·∇W_ε(x_i−x_j)` with a specified smooth pressure map. Antisymmetry proves total linear-momentum conservation; radial central forces prove angular-momentum conservation. An energy-conserving semidiscrete variant must include exactly matched internal-energy equation; dissipative artificial viscosity must have the stated sign and a separate energy inequality. Then prove local well-posedness and numerical time error for the **regularized finite particle system** on a collision-free/invariant controlled tube. Full compressible Euler shock convergence is not implied and is not silently asserted.\", \"proof_route\": \"**Local suppliers.** Kernel moment/Taylor bounds; particle-cell quadrature estimate; derivative kernel scaling; boundary moment-matrix conditioning; pair antisymmetry/central-force identities; smooth regularized-force Lipschitz bounds and time-integrator invariant/error ledger. Derivation from a stipulated discrete Lagrangian is mathematics, not an empirical validity claim. Explain which invariants are exact for the chosen time integrator: generic explicit RK does not conserve energy exactly.\", \"examples\": \"**B examples.** Interior polynomial reproduction; missing-neighbor boundary bias; disordered particle derivative inconsistency; two-particle equal/opposite force; pressure instability counterexample with invariant conservation still true.\"}",
      "items": [
        {
          "id": "def-meshfree-cell-quadrature-and-fill-radius",
          "kind": "definition",
          "title": "Meshfree cell quadrature and fill radius",
          "statement": "Let $\\Omega\\subseteq\\mathbb R^d$ be open. Write $B(x,r)$ for the open Euclidean ball from [[def-metric-ball]] with distance induced by [[def-euclidean-inner-product]]. A family of cells is **locally finite** here when every compact subset of $\\Omega$ meets only finitely many cells. A bounded set is **Jordan measurable** when its Jordan inner and outer contents agree ([[def-jordan-inner-and-outer-content]]). A **cell particle rule** is a nonempty locally finite family of bounded Jordan-measurable cells $C_j\\subseteq\\Omega$ whose interiors are disjoint and whose union covers $\\Omega$ up to their shared boundaries, together with nodes $x_j\\in C_j$ and positive volumes $V_j=\\int 1_{C_j}$. The cell volume integral is Riemann-defined and equals the Jordan content ([[def-riemann-integral-over-a-jordan-set]]). Its fill radius is $h=\\sup_j\\sup_{y\\in C_j}|y-x_j|$, using the Euclidean norm from [[def-euclidean-inner-product]]; require $0<h<\\infty$. Assume finite constants $C_V,C_L>0$ satisfy $V_j\\le C_Vh^d$ and the local volume bound\n\n$$\\sum_{j:C_j\\cap B(x,r)\\ne\\varnothing}V_j\\le C_L(r+h)^d$$\n\nfor every $x\\in\\mathbb R^d$ and $r>0$. For continuous $g:\\mathbb R^d\\to\\mathbb R$ with compact support contained in $\\Omega$ (support as in [[def-support-and-compactly-supported-riemann-integral-in-rn]]), define $Q_hg=\\sum_jV_jg(x_j)$; local finiteness makes this sum finite. At kernel width $\\varepsilon$, an evaluation point is **interior** when $B(x,R\\varepsilon+2h)\\subset\\Omega$. For periodic calculations, use the periodic extensions of the field and particle rule on $\\mathbb R^d$, so no kernel support is truncated.\n\nThe cell rule is integration-compatible: for every continuous $g:\\mathbb R^d\\to\\mathbb R$ with compact support contained in $\\Omega$, only finitely many cells meet $\\operatorname{supp}(g)$, every cell integral appearing below is Riemann-defined, and\n$$\\int_\\Omega g(y)\\,dy=\\sum_{j:C_j\\cap\\operatorname{supp}(g)\\ne\\varnothing}\\int_{C_j}g(y)\\,dy.$$\nThis finite partition identity is part of the quadrature data.",
