      "companion": "substructuring-feti-dp-and-bddc-preconditioners-examples",
      "requires": [
        "overlapping-schwarz-and-stable-subspace-decompositions"
      ],
      "description": "{\"scope\": \"Define discrete harmonic extension, interface Schur complement, primal assembled versus partially assembled interface spaces, primal constraints (corners/edge averages as appropriate), scaled averaging and a concrete BDDC method. Define corresponding FETI-DP dual multipliers/jump operator with explicit redundant-constraint convention. Prove well-definedness after removing local floating-subdomain nullspaces and establishing primal constraints; prove exact interface equivalence and the appropriate common nontrivial-spectrum comparison. Prove the classical bound C(1+log(H/h))² only for a stated scalar second-order diffusion family on shape-regular polygonal subdomains in 2D with matching quasiuniform triangulations and the selected corner/edge constraints/scaling; coefficient jumps need precisely stated subdomain-constant aligned coefficient/scaling assumptions. A 3D claim must add its edge/face constraint and trace proof. Do not claim arbitrary graph/mesh/high-contrast scalability.\", \"proof_route\": \"Reuse block elimination for Schur algebra. Prove harmonic extension minimizes energy, then characterize the BDDC preconditioner by constrained local minimization and averaging. Prove averaging energy bound using the exact discrete trace/H1/2 norm and vertex-cutoff logarithmic estimate; this logarithmic estimate, local trace lifting and stable coarse constraint construction are same-page analytic prerequisites with full proofs, not assumed universal inequalities. Show dual jump/averaging decomposition identities and map eigenvectors between primal and dual operators, explicitly excluding null/unit modes where required. Work/storage counts are separate from conditioning and depend on local/coarse solver costs.\", \"examples\": \"two floating Poisson subdomains, missing corner singularity, matching primal/dual spectrum, coarse-solve work bottleneck.\"}",
      "items": [
  {
    "id": "def-pe26-scalar-substructuring-model",
    "kind": "definition",
    "title": "Scalar finite-element substructuring model",
    "deps": [
      "def-matching-affine-simplicial-meshes",
      "def-broken-and-conforming-polynomial-spaces",
      "def-reference-finite-element-and-unisolvent-dofs"
    ],
    "justified_by": [],
    "dependency_level": 0,
    "statement": "Fix a bounded, connected polygonal open set $Ω\\subset\\mathbb R^2$ with a nonempty Dirichlet boundary segment. A finite **subdomain partition** is a family of connected polygonal open sets $Ω_i$, $1\\le i\\le N$, whose closures cover $\\overline\\Omega$, whose interiors are disjoint, and for which $\\overline\\Omega_i\\cap\\overline\\Omega_j$ ($i\\ne j$) is a whole common side, a common vertex, or empty. The outer Dirichlet segment is a union of mesh boundary sides. The edge-adjacency graph of each triangulated subdomain is connected, and the subdomain adjacency graph is connected to the Dirichlet boundary. Each subdomain has a matching affine simplicial triangulation; the triangulations match on every shared side. The family has a fixed shape-regularity bound, quasiuniform mesh size $h_i$, uniformly bounded polygon side count, corner angles bounded below, inradius comparable to $H_i$, and a uniform interior-cone parameter. Write $H_i=\\operatorname{diam}(\\Omega_i)$ and $H/h=\\max_i H_i/h_i$.\n\nUse continuous piecewise-affine ($P_1$) scalar functions on each triangulation. The reference-element and face-matching conventions are those of [[def-reference-finite-element-and-unisolvent-dofs]], [[def-matching-affine-simplicial-meshes]], and [[def-broken-and-conforming-polynomial-spaces]]: local polynomials are affine, their nodal values determine them, and conforming functions have equal traces across a shared side. For each subdomain choose a coefficient $\\rho_i>0$, constant on $\\Omega_i$ but allowed to jump across interfaces. Its local energy is\n\n$$a_i(v,w)=\\rho_i\\sum_{K\\subset\\Omega_i}\\int_K\\nabla v\\cdot\\nabla w\\,dx.$$\n\nThe global finite-element space consists of the continuous piecewise-affine functions on the subdomain meshes that vanish on the outer Dirichlet boundary, with energy $a(v,w)=\\sum_i a_i(v|_{\\Omega_i},w|_{\\Omega_i})$. Local interior degrees of freedom are the mesh vertices strictly inside $\\Omega_i$; local boundary degrees of freedom are its remaining vertices, with outer Dirichlet values fixed to zero. A **floating local block** means the same local energy before any outer-boundary values are fixed. A **subdomain corner** is a vertex of its polygonal boundary.\n\nFor uniform estimates, the fixed polygon-shape parameters include the scaled Lipschitz character: after rescaling each subdomain to diameter one, its boundary has a graph-chart cover with uniformly bounded chart count and Lipschitz constants and a uniformly positive neighborhood radius. The scaled mean-zero Poincaré constants are also included among these fixed shape parameters. A pointwise interior-cone bound alone is not a substitute for this uniform boundary-chart data. All constants in later two-dimensional estimates may depend on these polygon-shape, side-count, and mesh shape-regularity bounds, but not on $h$, $H$, the number of subdomains, or the positive numbers $\\rho_i$.",
    "source_path": "items/def-pe26-scalar-substructuring-model.md",
    "provenance": {
