          "url": "https://people.tamu.edu/~guermond/M661_FALL_2025/chap37.pdf"
        },
        {
          "title": "Mika Juntunen and Rolf Stenberg, Nitsche's method for general boundary conditions, Mathematics of Computation 78 (2009), 1353–1374",
          "url": "https://aaltodoc.aalto.fi/bitstreams/f6ebc2d5-8166-479a-96c8-45d2538ce179/download"
        },
        {
          "title": "Leszek F. Demkowicz, Lecture Notes on Energy Spaces",
          "url": "https://users.ices.utexas.edu/~leszek/classes/CSE393/book.pdf",
          "locator": "§3.1, p. 50, “Fractional Sobolev spaces on an arbitrary domain” (restriction definition and minimum-energy extension norm)."
        }
      ]
    },
    "statement": "Assume Countable Choice. Let $s\\ge0$ and let $D\\subset\\mathbb R^d$ be a nonempty open set. Use $H^s(\\mathbb R^d)$ with the Bessel-potential completion convention of [[def-real-order-bessel-potential-sobolev-space]]. Realize this completion in $L^2(\\mathbb R^d)$ by the canonical injection\n$$\\iota_sU:=\\mathcal F_2^{-1}\\bigl(\\langle\\xi\\rangle^{-s}J_sU\\bigr),$$\nwhere $J_s:H^s(\\mathbb R^d)\\to L^2(\\mathbb R^d)$ is the weighted Fourier isometry of [[thm-bessel-potential-completions-embed-in-tempered-distributions]] and $\\mathcal F_2$ is the unitary transform of [[thm-plancherel]]. Since $s\\ge0$, multiplication by $\\langle\\xi\\rangle^{-s}$ is bounded on $L^2$ with norm at most one and is injective. Hence $\\iota_s$ is linear, injective, and satisfies $\\|\\iota_sU\\|_2\\le\\|U\\|_{H^s}$; it sends each canonical Schwartz class to that function's almost-everywhere class. Equivalently, for a Cauchy representative $[u_j]$, $\\iota_s[u_j]=\\lim_j u_j$ in $L^2$, because $\\widehat u_j=\\langle\\xi\\rangle^{-s}J_su_j$ converges in $L^2$ and $\\mathcal F_2^{-1}$ is continuous. Identify $U$ with this $L^2$ class, and write $U|_D:=(\\iota_sU)|_D$. Define the **restriction space**\n$$H^s(D):=\\{U|_D:U\\in H^s(\\mathbb R^d)\\},\\qquad \\|u\\|_{H^s(D)}:=\\inf\\{\\|U\\|_{H^s(\\mathbb R^d)}:U|_D=u\\text{ a.e. on }D\\}.$$\nTwo extensions represent the same element exactly when their restrictions agree almost everywhere on $D$. This is the convention for fractional-order spaces on the polyhedral domain and its affine simplex cells used in the Nitsche items on this page. No $C^1$ boundary or trace theorem is part of this definition."
  },
  {
