      ],
      "description": "{\"scope\": \"Define SUPG for stationary convection-diffusion-reaction with explicit inflow/boundary conditions, tau_K scaling, diffusion epsilon>0, and c-div(b)/2 bounded below. Prove consistency, coercivity in the stated streamline-diffusion norm and mesh-dependent error bound retaining epsilon/tau/regularity. Define PSPG/grad-div for equal-order flow and prove a concrete stable bilinear norm estimate under a proved pressure stabilization condition. Define VMS as an exact resolved/unresolved algebraic decomposition and one residual closure; a closure/model assertion is not a proven universal turbulence model.\", \"proof_route\": \"Integrate transport by parts with inflow boundary sign; absorb element Laplacian cross terms by NA28 inverse estimate and tau restriction. Bound consistency using the actual residual and interpolate in the stabilized norm. For equal-order pressure control prove the pressure-gradient/local projection lemma and mixed stability; grad-div supplies an additional divergence penalty but does not by itself repair pressure inf-sup. VMS Schur complement follows block elimination; approximation of unresolved inverse has its own defect bound.\", \"examples\": \"convection boundary layer, overlarge stabilization, equal-order pressure oscillation, grad-div pressure sensitivity.\"}",
      "items": [
  {
    "id": "def-supg-one-dimensional-convection-diffusion-model",
    "kind": "definition",
    "title": "One-dimensional stationary convection–diffusion model and uniform P1 space",
    "deps": [],
    "statement": "Fix an integer $N\\ge1$, put $h=1/N$, and let $x_j=jh$ for $j=0,\\ldots,N$. The cells are $K_j=[x_{j-1},x_j]$. For $g_0,g_1\\in\\mathbb R$, let $S_h(g_0,g_1)$ be the finite-dimensional affine space of continuous functions on $[0,1]$ that are affine on each cell and have endpoint values $g_0,g_1$. Write $V_h=S_h(0,0)$ for the homogeneous test and trial vector space; explicitly, $S_h(g_0,g_1)=\\ell+V_h$ with $\\ell(x)=(1-x)g_0+xg_1$. “P1” means polynomial degree at most one on each interval.\n\nLet $\\epsilon>0$, $b\\in\\mathbb R\\setminus\\{0\\}$, and $f\\in C([0,1])$. The stationary boundary-value problem is to find $u\\in C^2([0,1])$ such that $-\\epsilon u''+b u'=f$ on $(0,1)$, with prescribed endpoint values $u(0)=g_0$ and $u(1)=g_1$. Its dimensionless cell Péclet number is $\\mathrm{Pe}=|b|h/(2\\epsilon)>0$. The stability theorem on this page uses the homogeneous branch $g_0=g_1=0$; the general endpoint notation also permits the boundary-layer example.",
    "provenance": {
