    "statement": "Let $\\Omega^-$ be a bounded connected $C^2$ domain in $\\mathbb R^3$ with connected boundary $\\Gamma$, let $\\Omega^+=\\mathbb R^3\\setminus\\overline{\\Omega^-}$ be connected, and let $n$ point outward from $\\Omega^-$. Fix $k>0$ and $\\eta\\in\\mathbb R\\setminus\\{0\\}$. The outgoing Helmholtz kernel is\n$$G_k(x,y)=\\frac{e^{ik|x-y|}}{4\\pi|x-y|},\\qquad x\\ne y.$$\nFor $\\phi\\in C^\\infty(\\Gamma)$ and $x\\notin\\Gamma$, define $S_k\\phi(x)=\\int_\\Gamma G_k(x,y)\\phi(y)\\,dS_y$ and $D_k\\phi(x)=\\int_\\Gamma\\partial_{n_y}G_k(x,y)\\phi(y)\\,dS_y$. The **combined-field potential** is $u_\\phi=D_k\\phi-i\\eta S_k\\phi$, and its exterior trace defines the smooth-density operator $A_{k,\\eta}\\phi=\\gamma^+u_\\phi$. This definition makes no assertion that $A_{k,\\eta}$ extends boundedly or Fredholmly to a Sobolev space; these analytical properties are proved in separate theorems. For this smooth-boundary branch, $\\gamma^+$ denotes the trace from $\\Omega^+$ and uses the same fixed normal $n$ as the interior trace. For this $C^2$ surface, fix a finite graph atlas and subordinate partition as in the general Lipschitz chart-space convention of [[def-bem-lipschitz-polyhedral-boundary-and-trace-spaces]], using graph surface measure. Use its square-sum Hilbert norm, real spaces, complexification and conjugate-dual convention for $H^{\\pm1/2}(\\Gamma)$. This imports the space convention only; the $C^2$ trace/lift and layer theorems are proved separately and do not invoke the polyhedral trace theorem.",
