      "description": "{\"scope\": \"Define compressible Navier–Stokes–Fourier as a mathematical balance law with positive density/temperature, ideal-gas constitutive functions, nonnegative shear/bulk viscosity and heat conductivity, and specified periodic or entropy-compatible wall boundaries. Derive the continuous entropy identity for smooth positive states. Define a concrete mixed gradient/viscous DG or SBP flux discretization with entropy variables. Prove consistency, global conservation under periodic data and semidiscrete entropy dissipation under the stated positive-semidefinite viscous matrix and boundary flux. Define one finite fully discrete algorithm using the time agent's method; positivity and fully discrete entropy are separate theorems, and may require PE03 limiting plus a proved compatible time discretization. Prove a smooth-solution consistency/error estimate on a compact positive-state tube only when the exact discrete coercivity/linearized stability bound is proved. Do not claim arbitrary-shock or global 3D continuum convergence.\", \"proof_route\": \"Differentiate the mathematical entropy and express viscous flux as K(U) grad(v), deriving symmetry/positive semidefiniteness with its density/temperature factors. Sum local integration by parts, pair the gradient equation with viscous variables, and complete dissipative volume/face squares; compute wall entropy flux explicitly. Interpolate a smooth solution and derive the discrete error identity, controlling nonlinear coefficients by tube bounds, scheme coercivity and Gronwall. Positivity cannot be inferred from entropy dissipation alone; prove the selected limiter/time-step compatibility or retain it as a mandatory unresolved authoring obligation before claiming a fully discrete theorem.\", \"examples\": \"periodic viscous manufactured solution, Couette/thermal wall balance, inviscid-versus-viscous entropy budget, near-vacuum failure.\"}",
