  {
    "id": "def-periodic-pic-grid-and-inner-product",
    "kind": "definition",
    "deps": [],
    "dependency_level": 0,
    "title": "Periodic uniform PIC grid and inner product",
    "justified_by": [],
    "provenance": {
      "statement": "ai-altered",
      "proof": "not-applicable"
    },
    "sources": {
      "references": [
        {
          "title": "R. W. Hockney and J. W. Eastwood, Computer Simulation Using Particles, Chapters 2, 4, and 5",
          "url": "https://djvu.online/file/cjOfwRgltjv1g"
        },
        {
          "title": "A. Myers, P. Colella, and B. Van Straalen, A 4th-Order Particle-in-Cell Method with Phase-Space Remapping for the Vlasov–Poisson Equation, SIAM Journal on Scientific Computing 39(3) (2017)",
          "url": "https://escholarship.org/content/qt2tt7x68b/qt2tt7x68b.pdf"
        }
      ]
    },
    "statement": "Fix a spatial dimension $d\\in\\{1,2,3\\}$, side length $L>0$, and odd integer $N\\ge3$. Put $h=L/N$ and $\\mathbb T_L^d=(\\mathbb R/L\\mathbb Z)^d$. The periodic grid has index set $J_N=(\\mathbb Z/N\\mathbb Z)^d$ and nodes $x_j=hj$ modulo $L$. A grid scalar is a real array $u=(u_j)_{j\\in J_N}$; a grid vector has $d$ real components at each node. Define\n$$ \\langle u,v\\rangle_h=h^d\\sum_{j\\in J_N}u_jv_j,\\qquad \\|u\\|_h=\\langle u,u\\rangle_h^{1/2},\\qquad \\bar u=L^{-d}h^d\\sum_{j\\in J_N}u_j . $$ The same pairing is componentwise for grid vectors. For the coordinate vector $e_a$, define the centered field difference and positive periodic Poisson stencil by $$ (D_a^0u)_j=\\frac{u_{j+e_a}-u_{j-e_a}}{2h},\\qquad (A_hu)_j=\\sum_{a=1}^d\\frac{2u_j-u_{j+e_a}-u_{j-e_a}}{h^2}. $$\nAll indices wrap modulo $N$. The discrete Fourier index set is $\\mathcal K_N=\\{-\\frac{N-1}{2},\\ldots,\\frac{N-1}{2}\\}^d$; the character at $k\\in\\mathcal K_N$ is $j\\mapsto e^{2\\pi i k\\cdot j/N}$. This odd-grid convention has no Nyquist mode."
  },
  {
