    "dependency_level": 2,
    "statement": "Let $A=D\\Phi$ and $A_h=D\\Phi_h$ be orientation-preserving cell-map derivatives with $\\det A,\\det A_h\\ge j_*>0$, $\\|A\\|_{op},\\|A_h\\|_{op}\\le M$, and $\\|A-A_h\\|_{op}\\le e_1$. For every unit reference-facet normal $\\widehat n$, define the surface Jacobians $J_\\Gamma(A)=\\|\\operatorname{cof}(A)\\widehat n\\|$, $J_\\Gamma(A_h)=\\|\\operatorname{cof}(A_h)\\widehat n\\|$, and mapped unit normals $n_\\Gamma(A)=\\operatorname{cof}(A)\\widehat n/J_\\Gamma(A)$ and $n_\\Gamma(A_h)=\\operatorname{cof}(A_h)\\widehat n/J_\\Gamma(A_h)$. Then\n$$\\|\\operatorname{cof}(A)-\\operatorname{cof}(A_h)\\|_{op}+|J_\\Gamma(A)-J_\\Gamma(A_h)|+|n_\\Gamma(A)-n_\\Gamma(A_h)|\\le C(d,M,j_*)e_1.$$\nFor any regular reference-facet parametrization $\\widehat f$, the chart Gram-metric factor of [[def-isoparametric-curved-cell-map]] is $\\sqrt{\\det(D\\widehat f^T A^T A D\\widehat f)}=J_\\Gamma(A)\\sqrt{\\det(D\\widehat f^T D\\widehat f)}$, pointwise with $A=D\\Phi(\\widehat f)$. Thus $J_\\Gamma(A)$ is the surface-area factor relative to reference-facet area; it equals the chart factor when the reference chart has unit area factor. The same relation holds for $A_h$.",
