    "dependency_level": 3,
    "statement": "Assume the Axiom of Choice and Countable Choice. Let $\\widehat\\Omega=(-1,1)^2$, let $\\Phi(x,y)=(x,y+x^2/4)$, and let $\\Phi_h$ be its continuous piecewise-affine nodal interpolant on a shape-regular matching triangular mesh with maximum diameter $h\\le1$, small enough that $J_h=\\det D\\Phi_h\\ge1/2$ as in [[lem-parabolic-domain-map-approximation]]. Put $J=\\det D\\Phi$ and define the pulled-back Laplace tensors $$B=J D\\Phi^{-1}D\\Phi^{-T},\\qquad B_h=J_hD\\Phi_h^{-1}D\\Phi_h^{-T}.$$ On $V=H^1_0(\\widehat\\Omega)$ set $$a(v,w)=\\int_{\\widehat\\Omega}\\nabla v^TB\\nabla w,\\qquad a_h(v,w)=\\int_{\\widehat\\Omega}\\nabla v^TB_h\\nabla w.$$ Let $V_h$ be the finite-dimensional space of continuous functions on $\\overline{\\widehat\\Omega}$ that are affine on each triangle and vanish on $\\partial\\widehat\\Omega$, viewed as a subspace of $V$. Suppose $a$ and $a_h$ are uniformly bounded and coercive on $V$ and $V_h$, respectively, with constants independent of $h$, $F\\in V^*$ and $F_h\\in V_h^*$ are bounded, $u\\in H^2(\\widehat\\Omega)\\cap V$ is real-valued and satisfies $a(u,w)=F(w)$ for every $w\\in V$, and $u_h\\in V_h$ satisfies $a_h(u_h,w_h)=F_h(w_h)$ for every $w_h\\in V_h$.\n\nIf a common physical boundary lift $G\\in C^2$ with bounded first and second derivatives on a convex neighborhood of the two geometry images is used, let $\\ell_{\\partial,h}$ be its pulled-back residual from [[lem-curved-boundary-lift-residual]] and set $\\epsilon_{\\partial,h}=\\|\\ell_{\\partial,h}\\|_{V^*}$. Assume the load difference on $V_h$ splits as $$ (F-F_h)|_{V_h}=L_{\\mathrm{bulk},h}+\\ell_{\\partial,h}|_{V_h},\\qquad \\|L_{\\mathrm{bulk},h}\\|_{V_h^*}\\le\\epsilon_{F,h}^{\\mathrm{bulk}},\\qquad \\epsilon_{F,h}^{\\mathrm{bulk}}\\ge0.$$ Here the $V^*$ and $V_h^*$ norms are induced by the $H^1$ norm. If no lift is used, set $\\ell_{\\partial,h}=0$ and take the same bound for the full load difference. Then $$\\|u-u_h\\|_{H^1(\\widehat\\Omega)}\\le C\\bigl(h\\|u\\|_{H^2(\\widehat\\Omega)}+\\epsilon_{F,h}^{\\mathrm{bulk}}+\\epsilon_{\\partial,h}\\bigr).$$ where $C$ is independent of $h$. For the parabolic geometry family, $\\epsilon_{\\partial,h}\\le C_G(e_0+e_1)\\le C_Gh$.",
