      "requires": [
        "spectral-collocation-and-galerkin-methods",
        "boundary-integral-equations-and-galerkin-bem"
      ],
      "description": "{\"scope\": \"Define exterior Helmholtz radiation condition and obstacle boundary data for a specified smooth/Lipschitz obstacle subclass. Prove uniqueness via Rellich identity/outgoing expansion and existence for the chosen combined-field or truncated variational formulation. Define local impedance absorbing boundaries and an exact modal Dirichlet-to-Neumann map for a sphere/circle; prove the actual truncation/reflection error. Define complex-stretch PML first on a Cartesian constant-coefficient mode branch; prove perfect matching and exponential decay there. A finite PML FEM convergence theorem requires the stated obstacle/stretch/parameter stability result and a separate exponential truncation bound; do not universalize the plane-wave calculation to every material or grazing/evanescent mode.\", \"proof_route\": \"Green imaginary part and outgoing expansion prove Rellich uniqueness; combined-field jump relations + Fredholm give existence. Modal separation computes DtN and artificial-boundary reflection. Complex-coordinate substitution computes matching, including branch of longitudinal wave number; bound damping away from the declared excluded angles, then coercive/inf-sup perturbation for truncation and discretization. State Helmholtz pollution restrictions on kh and degree explicitly for any error theorem.\", \"examples\": \"outgoing sphere, impedance reflection, PML normal/grazing incidence, resonance at insufficient damping.\"}",
      "items": [
        {
          "id": "def-pe18-cartesian-complex-stretch-pml",
          "kind": "definition",
          "title": "Cartesian complex-stretch PML for a homogeneous Helmholtz mode",
          "deps": [],
          "dependency_level": 0,
          "statement": "Let $k>0$, $x\\in\\mathbb R$ be the Cartesian normal coordinate, and $y\\in\\mathbb R^2$ the tangential coordinate. Let $\\sigma$ be nonnegative, continuous, piecewise $C^1$, and zero for $x\\le0$. Define\n$$S(x)=\\int_0^x\\sigma(t)\\,dt,\\qquad X(x)=x+iS(x),\\qquad s(x)=X'(x)=1+i\\sigma(x).$$\nThe pulled-back constant-coefficient Helmholtz operator in the layer is\n$$\\frac1{s(x)}\\partial_x\\left(\\frac1{s(x)}\\partial_x\\right)+\\Delta_y+k^2.$$\nThis definition concerns the homogeneous Cartesian branch only; it makes no claim for heterogeneous media, curved stretches, grazing or evanescent uniform attenuation, discretizations, or finite-element convergence.",
          "local_order": 1,
          "justified_by": [],
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "literature-derived",
            "proof": "not-applicable"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "Steven G. Johnson, Notes on Perfectly Matched Layers (PMLs), §§3.1–3.2",
                "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/2108.05348"
              },
              {
                "title": "Michler, Demkowicz, Kurtz, and Pardo, Improving the performance of Perfectly Matched Layers by means of hp-adaptivity, §§2.1–2.2",
                "url": "https://www.cs.ox.ac.uk/people/christian.michler/Homepage%20of%20Christian%20Michler/Publications_files/MicDemKurPar2007NMPDE.pdf"
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "def-pe18-local-impedance-absorbing-boundary",
          "kind": "definition",
          "title": "First-order local impedance condition at an artificial plane",
          "deps": [
            "def-pe18-exterior-helmholtz-problem"
          ],
          "dependency_level": 1,
          "statement": "Use the outgoing time convention $e^{-i\\omega t}$. On an artificial boundary with outward unit normal $n$, the first-order local impedance condition is $\\partial_nu-iku=0$. For the plane $x=R$ with $n=+e_x$, this is $\\partial_xu-iku=0$. It is the exact outgoing relation at normal incidence and an approximation for oblique propagating modes; no exactness or uniform small-reflection claim at grazing incidence is part of this definition.",
          "local_order": 2,
          "justified_by": [],
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "literature-derived",
            "proof": "not-applicable"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "Galkowski, Lafontaine, and Spence, Local absorbing boundary conditions on fixed domains give order-one errors for high-frequency waves",
                "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/2101.02154"
              },
              {
                "title": "NIST Digital Library of Mathematical Functions, Chapter 10: Bessel Functions",
                "url": "https://dlmf.nist.gov/10"
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "thm-pe18-pml-perfect-matching-and-propagating-mode-decay",
          "kind": "theorem",
          "title": "Perfect matching and attenuation for non-grazing propagating modes",
          "deps": [
            "def-pe18-cartesian-complex-stretch-pml"
          ],
          "dependency_level": 1,
          "statement": "Let $k>0$, $\\eta\\in\\mathbb R^2$ satisfy $|\\eta|<k$, and let $q=\\sqrt{k^2-|\\eta|^2}>0$ be the outgoing normal branch. In the stretch of [[def-pe18-cartesian-complex-stretch-pml]],\n$$u(x,y)=e^{iqX(x)+i\\eta\\cdot y}$$\nsolves the pulled-back Helmholtz equation. At $x=0$, its value and normal flux $(1/s)\\partial_xu$ agree with those of the physical mode $e^{iqx+i\\eta\\cdot y}$. Its modulus is $e^{-qS(x)}$. If a family of modes satisfies $q\\ge q_0>0$, then $|u(x,y)|\\le e^{-q_0S(x)}$ uniformly; whenever $S(x)\\ge\\sigma_0x$ for some $\\sigma_0>0$, this is at most $e^{-q_0\\sigma_0x}$. No uniform bound is asserted as $q\\downarrow0$.",
          "local_order": 3,
          "justified_by": [],
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "literature-derived",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "Steven G. Johnson, Notes on Perfectly Matched Layers (PMLs), §§3.1–3.2",
                "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/2108.05348"
              },
              {
                "title": "Michler, Demkowicz, Kurtz, and Pardo, Improving the performance of Perfectly Matched Layers by means of hp-adaptivity, §§2.1–2.2",
                "url": "https://www.cs.ox.ac.uk/people/christian.michler/Homepage%20of%20Christian%20Michler/Publications_files/MicDemKurPar2007NMPDE.pdf"
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "lem-pe18-spherical-hankel-modulus-and-radiation-bounds",
          "kind": "lemma",
          "title": "Spherical Hankel modulus and uniform radial bounds",
          "deps": [
            "def-spherical-bessel-and-hankel-functions",
            "lem-spherical-bessel-and-hankel-radial-solutions"
          ],
          "dependency_level": 2,
          "statement": "For integers $\\ell\\ge0$ and real $z>0$, write $h_\\ell=h_\\ell^{(1)}$ using [[def-spherical-bessel-and-hankel-functions]]. Then\n$$|h_\\ell(z)|^2=\\sum_{m=0}^{\\ell}S_{\\ell m}z^{-2m-2},\\qquad S_{\\ell m}=\\frac{(2m)!(\\ell+m)!}{4^m(m!)^2(\\ell-m)!}.$$\nConsequently $|h_\\ell(z)|$ is nonincreasing in $z$, $|h_{\\ell-1}(z)|\\le|h_\\ell(z)|$ for $\\ell\\ge1$, and $|h_\\ell'(z)|\\le(1+(\\ell+1)/z)|h_\\ell(z)|$. For every fixed $z_0>0$ and all $z\\ge z_0$,\n$$\\frac{|z(h_\\ell'(z)-ih_\\ell(z))|}{|h_\\ell(z_0)|}\\le(\\ell+1)e^{2z_0}.$$",
          "local_order": 4,
          "justified_by": [],
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "literature-derived",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "NIST Digital Library of Mathematical Functions, §§10.49–10.52",
                "url": "https://dlmf.nist.gov/10"
              },
              {
                "title": "Plamen Stefanov, Scattering and Inverse Scattering in R^n, graduate lecture notes",
                "url": "https://www.math.purdue.edu/~stefanov/publications/SCATTERING.pdf"
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "thm-pe18-planar-impedance-reflection-coefficient",
          "kind": "theorem",
          "title": "Exact plane-wave reflection coefficient of the local impedance condition",
          "deps": [
            "def-pe18-local-impedance-absorbing-boundary"
          ],
          "dependency_level": 2,
          "statement": "For $0<q\\le k$ and tangential wavevector $\\eta\\in\\mathbb R^2$ satisfying $q^2+|\\eta|^2=k^2$, consider\n$$u(x,y)=e^{i\\eta\\cdot y}\\left(e^{iq(x-R)}+\\mathcal R e^{-iq(x-R)}\\right).$$\nThe impedance condition [[def-pe18-local-impedance-absorbing-boundary]] gives\n$$\\mathcal R=\\frac{q-k}{q+k},\\qquad |\\mathcal R|=\\frac{k-q}{k+q}.$$\nThus reflection is zero at normal incidence $q=k$ and its magnitude tends to one as $q\\downarrow0$ (grazing incidence).",
          "local_order": 5,
          "justified_by": [],
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "literature-derived",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "Galkowski, Lafontaine, and Spence, Local absorbing boundary conditions on fixed domains give order-one errors for high-frequency waves",
                "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/2101.02154"
              },
              {
                "title": "Steven G. Johnson, Notes on Perfectly Matched Layers (PMLs), §2",
                "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/2108.05348"
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "thm-pe18-finite-pml-dirichlet-reflection",
          "kind": "theorem",
          "title": "Exact reflected-mode error from a finite Dirichlet-terminated PML",
          "deps": [
            "def-pe18-cartesian-complex-stretch-pml",
            "thm-pe18-pml-perfect-matching-and-propagating-mode-decay"
          ],
          "dependency_level": 2,
          "statement": "For $q>0$, $A\\ne0$, and a finite layer $0\\le x\\le L$ with $S(L)>0$, let a one-dimensional mode be $u(x)=Ae^{iqX(x)}+Be^{-iqX(x)}$, with the PML stretch from [[def-pe18-cartesian-complex-stretch-pml]]. Imposing $u(L)=0$ gives the reflected amplitude at the interface\n$$\\frac BA=-e^{2iqX(L)},\\qquad \\left|\\frac BA\\right|=e^{-2qS(L)}.$$\nThis is the exact continuous one-mode error for a Dirichlet termination.",
          "local_order": 6,
          "justified_by": [],
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "literature-derived",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "Steven G. Johnson, Notes on Perfectly Matched Layers (PMLs), §3.3",
                "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/2108.05348"
              },
              {
                "title": "Michler, Demkowicz, Kurtz, and Pardo, Improving the performance of Perfectly Matched Layers by means of hp-adaptivity, §§2.1–2.2",
                "url": "https://www.cs.ox.ac.uk/people/christian.michler/Homepage%20of%20Christian%20Michler/Publications_files/MicDemKurPar2007NMPDE.pdf"
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "def-pe18-exact-spherical-dtn-map",
          "kind": "definition",
          "title": "Exact modal Dirichlet-to-Neumann map on a sphere",
          "deps": [
            "def-three-dimensional-spherical-harmonic-space",
            "lem-three-dimensional-laplace-spherical-harmonic-addition-theorem",
            "def-spherical-bessel-and-hankel-functions",
            "lem-spherical-bessel-and-hankel-radial-solutions",
            "lem-pe18-spherical-hankel-radial-identities",
            "lem-pe18-spherical-hankel-modulus-and-radiation-bounds"
          ],
          "dependency_level": 3,
          "statement": "For $k>0$, $R>0$, and $g\\in C^\\infty(\\mathbb S^2;\\mathbb C)$, let $g_\\ell$ be its $L^2(\\mathbb S^2)$-orthogonal projection onto $\\mathcal H_\\ell$ and set\n$$\\tau_\\ell(k,R)=k\\frac{(h_\\ell^{(1)})'(kR)}{h_\\ell^{(1)}(kR)},\\qquad T_{k,R}g=\\sum_{\\ell=0}^\\infty\\tau_\\ell(k,R)g_\\ell.$$\nThe associated outgoing extension for $r\\ge R$ is\n$$v(r,\\omega)=\\sum_{\\ell=0}^\\infty\\frac{h_\\ell^{(1)}(kr)}{h_\\ell^{(1)}(kR)}g_\\ell(\\omega).$$\nThe denominator is nonzero by [[lem-pe18-spherical-hankel-radial-identities]]. The convergence, smoothness, PDE, and radiation properties are asserted in [[thm-pe18-exact-spherical-dtn-map-on-smooth-data]].",
          "justified_by": [
