          "statement": "Fix one finite-dimensional realization in [[def-phase-field-discrete-realizations]] and $T>0$. Let $G_{c,h}(u)=\\kappa A_hu+\\nabla_hP_{c,h}(u)$ and $G_{e,h}(u)=\\nabla_hP_{e,h}(u)$. The exact semidiscrete Allen–Cahn trajectory is the solution of $y'=-M(G_{c,h}(y)-G_{e,h}(y))$; the exact semidiscrete Cahn–Hilliard trajectory is the fixed-mean solution of $y'=-MA_h(G_{c,h}(y)-G_{e,h}(y))$. Assume the applicable exact trajectory is $C^2$ on $[0,T]$ and remains in a compact set. Let the numerical solution start from $u^0=y(0)$ and use the convex-splitting step. Then there are constants $C>0$ and $\\tau_0>0$, depending on the fixed spatial realization, $\\varepsilon,\\kappa,M$, the exact trajectory, and $T$, such that, for $0<\\tau\\le\\tau_0$:\n\n- for Allen–Cahn, $\\max_{n\\tau\\le T}\\|u^n-y(t_n)\\|_h\\le C\\tau$;\n- for Cahn–Hilliard, with $y$ and $u^n$ having the same mean and $\\|z\\|_{-1,h}^2=\\langle A_h^\\dagger z,z\\rangle_h$ on mean-zero vectors, $\\max_{n\\tau\\le T}\\|u^n-y(t_n)\\|_{-1,h}\\le C\\tau$.\n\nThis is a fixed-mesh time estimate; it is not a uniform spatial error bound.",
