  {
    "id": "lem-hp-triangle-degree-uniform-strong-edge-lift",
    "kind": "lemma",
    "title": "A degree-uniform polynomial edge lift with the strong edge norm",
    "deps": [
      "def-matching-affine-simplicial-meshes",
      "def-sobolev-space-wkp-and-its-norm",
      "lem-shape-regular-affine-simplex-scaling",
      "thm-fubini-theorem-for-l-one-on-sigma-finite-product-spaces",
      "thm-cauchy-schwarz-in-an-inner-product-space",
      "def-countable-choice"
    ],
    "justified_by": [],
    "statement": "Assume Countable Choice. Let $p\\in\\mathbb N_0$, let $T=\\{(x,y):x>0,\\ y>0,\\ x+y<1\\}$, and let $q$ be a real polynomial of degree at most $p$ satisfying $q(0)=q(1)=0$. Define the polynomial $R(t)=q(t)/t$ by cancellation at $t=0$, and put\n\n$$\nLq(x,y)=q(x)-xR(1-y).\n$$\n\nThen $Lq\\in\\mathbb P_p(T)$, it equals $q$ on $y=0$, and it vanishes on $x=0$ and $x+y=1$. Moreover,\n\n$$\n\\|Lq\\|_{H^1(T)}\\le4\\|q'\\|_{L^2(0,1)},\n$$\n\nindependently of $p$. Permuting the three vertices gives the same bound, up to one fixed constant, for each reference edge. If $K$ is a shape-regular affine triangle, $E$ is an edge of length $\\ell_E$, and $h_K=\\operatorname{diam}(K)$, the transported lift satisfies\n\n$$\n|L_Eq|_{H^1(K)}\\le C_\\gamma\\ell_E^{1/2}\\|\\partial_\\tau q\\|_{L^2(E)},\\qquad \\|L_Eq\\|_{L^2(K)}\\le C_\\gamma h_K\\ell_E^{1/2}\\|\\partial_\\tau q\\|_{L^2(E)}.\n$$\n\nConsequently continuous polynomial data on the three edges of a triangle, each of degree at most the cell degree, have a polynomial extension of that cell degree. The estimate uses the strong $H^1$ edge norm and asserts no optimal $H^{1/2}$ trace-lifting bound.",
    "provenance": {
      "statement": "ai-altered",
      "proof": "ai-altered"
    },
    "sources": {
      "references": [
        {
          "title": "Joachim Schöberl, Numerical Methods for Partial Differential Equations, Chapter 30: hp Finite Elements",
          "url": "https://jschoeberl.github.io/iFEM/pversion/hpfem.html",
          "locator": "§30.4.2, projection-based interpolation on triangles. The source states a p-stable H^(1/2) extension but leaves major steps as exercises; this item proves an explicit, different H^1-edge-norm lift."
