    "dependency_level": 2,
    "statement": "For every $p\\ge1$, the one-dimensional GLL rule integrates all polynomials of degree at most $2p-1$ exactly and has positive weights. If $f=\\sum_{n=0}^pa_nP_n$, then\n$$Q_p(f^2)-\\int_{-1}^1f^2=\\frac{2(p+1)}{p(2p+1)}a_p^2.$$\nFor every $v\\in\\mathbb Q_p([-1,1]^d)$,\n$$\\|v\\|_{L^2}^2\\le Q_p^{\\otimes d}(v^2)\\le3^d\\|v\\|_{L^2}^2,\\qquad \\|\\nabla v\\|_{L^2}^2\\le Q_p^{\\otimes d}(|\\nabla v|^2)\\le3^d\\|\\nabla v\\|_{L^2}^2.$$\nThe same inequalities hold on affine boxes under the transported rule. If a pointwise-defined scalar coefficient $a:\\overline K\\to\\mathbb R$ is Lebesgue measurable on $K$ and satisfies $0<\\underline a\\le a(x)\\le\\overline a$ throughout $\\overline K$, the sampled form $Q_{K,p}(a|\\nabla v|^2)$ is equivalent to the exact weighted energy $\\int_Ka|\\nabla v|^2$ with lower factor $\\underline a/\\overline a$ and upper factor $3^d\\overline a/\\underline a$. These are p-uniform stability bounds; they do not bound the Strang consistency defect or equate quadrature with exact Galerkin assembly.",
