    "justified_by": [],
    "dependency_level": 3,
    "statement": "Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice ([[def-countable-choice]]) for this Lebesgue-measurable-volume branch. Work on the periodic box $\\mathbb T^d_\\ell$ of [[def-semilagrangian-periodic-grid-and-interpolant]]. For measure calculations use the bounded open fundamental domain $\\Omega:=\\prod_{j=1}^d(0,\\ell_j)$; the period faces omitted from the half-open grid-indexing copy $Q=\\prod_{j=1}^d[0,\\ell_j)$ are Lebesgue-null by [[thm-lebesgue-measure-of-a-box-of-every-kind]]. For a periodic measurable set $E$, define $|E|:=\\lambda_d(E\\cap\\Omega)$, where $\\lambda_d$ is the $d$-dimensional Lebesgue measure of [[def-lebesgue-measure-and-the-lebesgue-sigma-algebra]]. Let $\\{C_j\\}_{j=1}^J$ be a finite measurable source-cell partition and $\\{A_i\\}_{i=1}^J$ a finite measurable arrival-cell partition of $\\Omega$ up to null sets, with every cell of positive volume; their grid labels use the half-open convention in $Q$. Let $\\Phi=\\Phi_{t_{n+1},t_n}$ be the exact characteristic map from time $t_n$ to $t_{n+1}$. Its inverse images $D_i:=\\Phi^{-1}(A_i)$ are the **departure cells**; [[lem-periodic-flow-departure-cells-have-positive-finite-volume]] gives their measurability and positive finite volume, while [[lem-periodic-characteristic-map-is-orientation-preserving]] gives the diffeomorphism and positive-Jacobian convention.\n\nGiven a source cell-average density $\\rho_j^n$ on $C_j$, set\n$$\\overline\\rho_i:=\\frac{1}{|D_i|}\\sum_{j=1}^J|D_i\\cap C_j|\\rho_j^n,\\qquad \\rho_i^{n+1}:=\\frac{|D_i|}{|A_i|}\\overline\\rho_i.$$\nThe first formula is the exact intersection-volume remap of [[def-intersection-volume-conservative-remapping]] from the source partition to the departure partition. The second is the characteristic volume factor that converts the average on a departure cell into the transported mass per arrival-cell volume. All intersections and volumes are exact in this mathematical definition; a geometric approximation or quadrature rule would require a separate error term.",
