      "description": "{\"scope\": \"Define monotone upwind and positive-interpolation semi-Lagrangian schemes for a specified first-order HJ/HJB Cauchy problem, and a discounted stationary Bellman equation on a periodic domain with compact or finite controls and bounded Lipschitz coefficients. Prove monotonicity, L∞ stability, consistency and numerical-scheme-to-viscosity convergence by a full half-relaxed-limit argument using the exact existing first-order comparison class. A control minimization over infinite controls is an explicit oracle or a finite control discretization with its own uniform Hamiltonian error. Define finite-control Howard/policy iteration for the assembled proper M-matrix Bellman system; prove monotone improvement and finite termination with deterministic tie handling/no switch on equal value, or state a separately proved contraction stopping bound. Include a concrete second-order Bellman controlled-diffusion scheme on a monotone diagonal-dominant/wide-stencil branch; prove its comparison/convergence with new second-order viscosity suppliers. No generic central stencil monotonicity for arbitrary anisotropic diffusion, no unproved universal convergence rate.\", \"proof_route\": \"Upwind/positive interpolation makes nodal update order preserving; positive discount gives stationary sup contraction and discrete comparison/unique solution. Finite policy matrices have verified M-matrix inverse positivity; compare successive value solutions, show each strict switch strictly improves at a component and eliminate repeated policies using tie rule, so finite policy space terminates. Derive consistency on smooth test functions including h²/dt interpolation and control-grid error where relevant. To pass numerical limits, choose strict touching test functions, extract near-maximal mesh nodes, apply discrete monotonicity replacing states by test-function values plus contact error, then take limsup/liminf; prove boundary/initial barriers and use continuous comparison to squeeze both limits. The published half-relaxed theorem applies to approximate PDE solutions, so this mesh-node consistency proof is genuinely new. For second order locally define semijets/degenerate ellipticity, prove the required theorem of sums via sup/inf convolution and twice-differentiability arguments (with actual local analytic suppliers), then doubling variables and discount produce comparison; prove Perron/barrier existence or obtain it from convergent discrete approximants. These large but established proofs belong here before their consumers, not a citation to Barles–Souganidis. Restrict a second-order theorem to the coefficient/boundary class for which all those estimates are supplied.\", \"examples\": \"eikonal/HJ kink, discounted control switching, monotone versus nonmonotone stencil, diagonal diffusion Bellman manufactured value, infinite-control quantization.\"}",
